var e=JSON.parse('[[201,"Pixel Perfect is an approach in which the markup matches the design mockup exactly. The main tools are browser extensions such as PerfectPixel (Chrome) and PixelPerfect (Firefox), along with the built-in developer tools. They let you overlay the mockup on the page and compare it pixel by pixel.","What Pixel Perfect is and why it is needed\\nPixel Perfect is a markup technique in which the final HTML/CSS code reproduces the design mockup (normally in PSD, Figma or Sketch format) as closely as possible. This matters for ensuring a consistent appearance on every device and browser, and for satisfying the requirements of the client or the designer.\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- PerfectPixel (Chrome) — an extension that lets you upload an image of the mockup and overlay it on the page with adjustable transparency.\\n- PixelPerfect (Firefox) — the equivalent for Firefox, with similar functionality.\\n- The built-in developer tools — in Chrome DevTools you can use the \\"Rendering\\" panel and the \\"Screenshot\\" option for comparison.\\n- Figma Mirror — for viewing the mockup on mobile devices in real time.\\nAn example of using PerfectPixel\\n\\nPractical tips\\n\\n- Always check your markup at several screen resolutions.\\n- Use CSS properties such as `box-sizing: border-box` in order to avoid discrepancies in dimensions.\\n- For complex mockups, apply grids (CSS Grid or Flexbox) for precise positioning.\\nConclusion: Pixel Perfect tools are necessary to frontend developers working in a team with designers, in order to minimize discrepancies between the mockup and the finished product. They are especially useful at the markup stage and when work is signed off.","// Install the PerfectPixel extension from the Chrome Web Store\\n// Open the page and click the extension\'s icon\\n// Upload an image of the mockup (design.png, for example)\\n// Adjust the opacity for comparison\\n// Use the arrow keys for precise positioning"],[202,"Pixel Perfect markup is an approach in which the finished HTML/CSS code matches the design mockup entirely, to the pixel. The developer uses tools to overlay the markup on the mockup and works until the dimensions, spacing, colours and fonts match perfectly. This demands attentiveness and skill with CSS.","What is Pixel Perfect markup?\\nPixel Perfect markup is a method of developing web interfaces in which the final HTML/CSS code is visually identical to the design mockup the designer provided. The main goal is to make every element (blocks, text, images) match to within a single pixel. This approach is often used in projects where a high degree of visual accuracy matters — in landing pages or corporate sites, for example.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe developer opens the mockup (normally in Figma, Sketch or Photoshop) and builds the page in the browser alongside it. For checking, special tools are used, such as browser extensions (PixelPerfect, for example) or plugins that overlay a semi-transparent image of the mockup on top of the markup. The developer then adjusts the CSS properties (width, height, margin, padding, font-size, color and so on) until they match exactly.\\nA code example\\nSuppose the mockup has a button 200x50 pixels in size, with 20px of spacing above it and a 16px Arial font. The markup will look like this:\\n\\nAfter that the developer checks whether the button matches the mockup and adjusts the values if necessary.\\nWhen should you apply it?\\nPixel Perfect markup is justified in projects with strict design requirements, where even a small deviation may be critical (branded sites, for example). In modern responsive and fluid layouts, however, this approach may be excessive, since priority is given to flexibility and ease of maintenance. Conclusion: use Pixel Perfect when accuracy matters more than development speed, but keep the trade-offs with responsiveness in mind.","<button class=\\"btn\\">Click me</button>\\n\\n.btn {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n  margin-top: 20px;\\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n  background-color: #007bff;\\n  color: #fff;\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n}"],[203,"The browser loads the HTML and CSS and parses them into the DOM and the CSSOM. It then combines them into the render tree, computes the elements\' geometry (layout) and draws the pixels on the screen (paint). This process is called the critical rendering path.","How the browser handles CSS\\nWhen the browser loads a web page, it processes the HTML and CSS in sequence. First the HTML is parsed, creating the DOM (Document Object Model). In parallel the CSS files are loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). These two trees are combined into the render tree, which contains only the visible elements.\\nThe rendering stages\\n\\n- Parsing the CSS: the browser takes the CSS rules apart, resolves conflicts (the cascade and specificity) and builds the CSSOM.\\n- Layout (reflow): the dimensions and positions of every element on the page are computed.\\n- Paint: the browser draws the pixels, filling in colours, shadows, borders and so on.\\n- Composite: the layers are combined into the final image.\\nAn example\\n\\nThe browser will create a CSSOM node for .box, then compute its dimensions during layout, and fill it with red during paint.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding this process helps you optimize performance: avoid frequent reflows and repaints, and use properties that do not cause the layout to be recomputed (transform rather than changing top/left, for example).","/* CSS */\\n.box {\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 100px;\\n  background: red;\\n}"],[204,"localStorage and sessionStorage normally have a limit of about 5-10 MB per domain, depending on the browser. Cookies are limited to 4 KB per cookie and a maximum of 20-50 cookies per domain. These limits matter when choosing the right storage for a web application.","The limits of the browser\'s storage mechanisms\\nThe browser\'s storage mechanisms, such as localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies, have strict size limits set by the browser vendors. These limits affect which data can be stored on the client without a loss of performance or errors.\\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage\\nBoth use the same limits, normally from 5 to 10 MB per domain. In Chrome and Firefox, for example, the limit is 5 MB, while in Opera it is up to 10 MB. If you exceed the limit, the browser throws a QuotaExceededError exception. These stores suit caching data but not large files.\\n\\nCookies\\nCookies have a far smaller limit: 4 KB per cookie and normally up to 20-50 cookies per domain (it depends on the browser). They are sent with every HTTP request, so their size is critical for performance. Cookies are better used only for authentication or identification, not for storing large amounts of data.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of storage depends on the task: for small amounts of data (up to 4 KB) cookies will do; for medium amounts (up to 5-10 MB), localStorage or sessionStorage. For large volumes, use server-side databases or IndexedDB, which has limits of up to several hundred MB.","try {\\n  localStorage.setItem(\'key\', \'value\');\\n} catch (e) {\\n  if (e.name === \'QuotaExceededError\') {\\n    console.log(\'The storage limit has been exceeded\');\\n  }\\n}"],[205,"A hidden element stays interactive when the property visibility: hidden or opacity: 0 is used. In the first case the element is invisible but still takes up space and responds to clicks. In the second the element is completely transparent but also stays clickable. With display: none the element is removed from the flow and loses its interactivity.","How hiding affects interactivity\\nCSS has several ways of hiding an element, and their behaviour differs. If you want the element to stay interactive (responding to clicks or focus, for example), you have to use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0`. With `display: none` the element is removed from the document\'s flow entirely and loses all interactivity.\\nCode examples\\n\\nIn this example the element is invisible, but a click in its place will trigger the handler. `opacity: 0` works in the same way — the element is transparent but stays in the flow and responds to events.\\nWhen this is useful\\n\\n- For creating appearance/disappearance animations without losing state.\\n- For hiding elements that should remain available to screen readers (with care).\\n- For implementing custom checkboxes or buttons, where a visually hidden element controls the behaviour.\\nConclusion: use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0` if you need to preserve the interactivity of a hidden element — for animations or accessibility, for example. For complete removal from the interface, apply `display: none`.","<div id=\\"hidden\\" style=\\"visibility: hidden;\\">Click me</div>\\n<script>\\n  document.getElementById(\'hidden\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    alert(\'The click worked!\');\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[206,"The building of the DOM can be blocked by synchronous scripts, which run immediately on loading, and also by CSS files if they load before the scripts. The browser pauses the parsing of the HTML until it has loaded and executed the script or processed the CSS. This slows the display of the page down.","What blocks the building of the DOM?\\nBuilding the DOM (Document Object Model) is the process in which the browser parses the HTML and creates a tree structure of elements. Some resources can pause this process, delaying the painting of the page.\\nThe main blockers\\n\\n- Synchronous scripts — `<script>` tags without the `async` or `defer` attributes. The browser stops parsing the HTML, loads the script, executes it, and only then carries on building the DOM.\\n- CSS files — although CSS does not block the DOM directly by itself, it can block the execution of scripts that follow it. The browser waits for the CSS to load before running the script, which delays the DOM indirectly.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow to avoid the blocking\\n\\n- Use `async` or `defer` for scripts so that they do not block the parsing.\\n- Put your CSS in `<head>` and your scripts at the end of `<body>`.\\n- Minimize the number of blocking resources.\\nConclusion: understanding what blocks the DOM helps you optimize a page\'s loading, especially for improving First Contentful Paint (FCP) and the overall user experience.","<!-- A blocking script -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script>\\n<!-- The parsing of the HTML is paused until script.js has loaded and run -->\\n<div>This element will appear later</div>"],[207,"The property display: none removes an element from the document\'s flow entirely; it takes up no space and is invisible. The property visibility: hidden makes an element invisible, but it carries on taking up space in the flow. Neither property removes the element from the DOM tree, but display: none excludes it from rendering.","The effect on the DOM and the document\'s flow\\nThe properties `display: none` and `visibility: hidden` affect an element\'s display and its place in the document differently. Neither removes the element from the DOM tree, but they change how it takes part in rendering.\\ndisplay: none\\nThe element is removed from the document\'s flow entirely. It takes up no space, and the neighbouring elements occupy its space. The element is invisible and takes no part in mouse or keyboard events.\\n\\nIn this example the second element is not displayed and takes up no space; the third element will come immediately after the first.\\nvisibility: hidden\\nThe element becomes invisible but carries on taking up space in the document\'s flow. It is not displayed, but its dimensions and position are preserved, affecting the placement of the other elements.\\n\\nHere the second element is not visible, but an empty space equal to its size remains between the first and the third.\\nUse\\n\\n- `display: none` is used to hide an element completely when its space has to be freed up (in responsive design, for example).\\n- `visibility: hidden` is applied when you need to hide an element but keep its space (for animations or as a placeholder, for example).\\nConclusion: the choice between these properties depends on the task — whether the element\'s space in the document\'s flow has to be preserved or whether it should be removed from the visual presentation entirely.","<div>The first element</div>\\n<div style=\\"display: none\\">A hidden element</div>\\n<div>The third element</div>\\n\\n<div>The first element</div>\\n<div style=\\"visibility: hidden\\">An invisible element</div>\\n<div>The third element</div>"],[208,"Hidden elements are generally not indexed by search engines if they are hidden completely with CSS (display: none). If an element is hidden but its content is still readable (through visibility: hidden, for example), however, search crawlers may index it. Using hidden elements to manipulate SEO can lead to penalties.","The effect of hidden elements on SEO\\nHidden elements on a web page can affect SEO differently depending on how they are hidden. Search engines such as Google aim to index content that is visible to users and may ignore hidden content or handle it differently.\\nThe main ways of hiding elements\\n\\n- display: none — the element is removed from the document\'s flow entirely and is not displayed. Search crawlers normally do not index such content, since it is considered invisible to users.\\n- visibility: hidden — the element stays in the flow but becomes invisible. Its content may be indexed, since it is still present in the DOM.\\n- opacity: 0 — the element is transparent but takes up space. Search engines may index its content, since it is readable.\\n- position: absolute; left: -9999px — the element is moved off the screen. This method is often used to hide text, but it can be judged an attempt to manipulate SEO.\\nA code example\\n\\nRecommendations\\nUse hidden elements only to improve the user experience — for modal windows or dropdown menus, for example. Do not try to hide keywords or links in order to manipulate your ranking, since that can lead to penalties from search engines.\\nConclusion: hidden elements should be used with care, since their effect on SEO depends on the method of hiding. It is best to avoid hiding important content and to concentrate on creating good, visible content for users.","<!-- A hidden element with display: none -->\\n<div style=\\"display: none;\\">This text will not be indexed</div>\\n\\n<!-- A hidden element with visibility: hidden -->\\n<div style=\\"visibility: hidden;\\">This text may be indexed</div>\\n\\n<!-- A hidden element with opacity: 0 -->\\n<div style=\\"opacity: 0;\\">This text may also be indexed</div>"],[209,"The browser receives an HTTP response consisting of a status line, headers and a body. The status line contains a code and a message — 200 OK, for example. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type. The body contains the data that was requested — the page\'s HTML code, for example.","The structure of an HTTP response\\nWhen the browser sends a request to the server, the server returns an HTTP response. That response consists of three main parts: the status line, the headers and the body. The status line includes the protocol version, the status code (200, 404, for example) and a text message. The headers contain metadata such as the content type, the length of the body, caching and other parameters. The response body is the actual data the browser processes — HTML, JSON or an image, for example.\\nAn example HTTP response\\nConsider a simple example in which the server returns an HTML page. The response will have the status line `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`, headers such as `Content-Type: text/html`, and a body with the HTML code. Here is how it might look as text:\\n\\nThe key components\\n\\n- The status line: it indicates whether the request succeeded or failed. 200 OK, for example, means success; 404 Not Found means the resource was not found.\\n- The headers: they carry additional information such as the data type (Content-Type), caching (Cache-Control) or cookies (Set-Cookie).\\n- The body: it contains the payload the browser displays or processes. It may be empty — with a 204 No Content response, for example.\\nPractical use\\nUnderstanding the structure of an HTTP response matters for debugging network requests, configuring servers and building APIs. When developing a REST API, for example, the server returns JSON in the response body with the appropriate Content-Type header. Here is an example in Node.js using Express:\\n\\nIn this example the server returns status 200, a Content-Type header and JSON in the body.\\nConclusion: the HTTP response is a fundamental element of web communication that you have to know in order to work with web technologies, debug and build server applications.","HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nContent-Type: text/html\\nContent-Length: 123\\n\\n<html>\\n<body>\\n<h1>Hello, World!</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\nconst express = require(\'express\');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.get(\'/api/data\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.status(200);\\n  res.set(\'Content-Type\', \'application/json\');\\n  res.json({ message: \'Success\', data: [1, 2, 3] });\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[210,"Semantic tags carry meaning, describing their content. Examples are `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<aside>` and `<footer>`. They improve accessibility for screen readers and help search engines understand a page\'s structure.","What are semantic HTML tags?\\nSemantic tags are HTML elements that describe their content and their role in the document\'s structure explicitly. Unlike the generic `<div>` or `<span>`, semantic tags carry meaning, which makes code more readable and comprehensible both to developers and to browsers, search engines and assistive technologies.\\nThe main HTML5 semantic tags\\n\\n- `<header>` — the introductory part or navigation for a section.\\n- `<nav>` — a block of navigation links.\\n- `<main>` — the page\'s main content (unique to the page).\\n- `<article>` — an independent, self-contained piece of content (an article, a post).\\n- `<section>` — a thematic group of content, normally with a heading.\\n- `<aside>` — content that is only indirectly related (a sidebar, a pull-quote).\\n- `<footer>` — the concluding part of a section or page.\\n- `<figure>` and `<figcaption>` — an illustration with a caption.\\n- `<time>` — a date or a time.\\n- `<mark>` — highlighted text (search results, for example).\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhy use semantic tags?\\n\\n- Accessibility: screen readers and other assistive technologies can interpret a page\'s structure better, helping users with disabilities.\\n- SEO: search engines (Google, Yandex) take semantics into account for ranking and for displaying snippets.\\n- Maintainability: the code becomes more readable and easier for a team of developers to maintain.\\n- The future: semantic tags are part of the HTML5 standard, supported by every modern browser.\\nConclusion: use semantic tags whenever you can, in order to improve accessibility, SEO and the readability of your code. They are especially useful in large projects and when working as a team.","<body>\\n  <header>\\n    <h1>My blog</h1>\\n    <nav><a href=\\"/\\">Home</a></nav>\\n  </header>\\n  <main>\\n    <article>\\n      <h2>The article\'s title</h2>\\n      <p>The article\'s text...</p>\\n      <time datetime=\\"2025-03-20\\">20 March 2025</time>\\n    </article>\\n    <aside>\\n      <p>Advertising or links</p>\\n    </aside>\\n  </main>\\n  <footer>&copy; 2025</footer>\\n</body>"],[211,"The semantic tags header, section, article and footer are used to mark up a page\'s content logically. header defines the introductory part or the navigation, section groups thematically related content, article represents a self-contained block (a post or a news item, for example), and footer contains information about the author, a copyright notice or links. They improve the readability of code and help search engines and screen readers.","The purpose of the semantic tags\\nThe semantic tags `<header>`, `<section>`, `<article>` and `<footer>` were introduced in HTML5 in order to give a web page\'s structure real meaning rather than simply using the generic `<div>`. They help browsers, search engines and assistive technologies (screen readers, for example) interpret the content correctly.\\nA description of each tag\\n\\n- <header> — represents the introductory part of a section or of the whole page. It normally contains a logo, a heading, navigation or a search box. It can be used several times on a page.\\n- <section> — groups thematically related content. Every `<section>` should have a heading (`<h1>`–`<h6>`, for example). It is used for logical blocks such as chapters, tabs or groups of news items.\\n- <article> — denotes a self-contained, independent fragment of content that could be distributed on its own (a blog article, a comment, a news item). An `<article>` may contain its own `<header>` and `<footer>`.\\n- <footer> — contains information about the author, a copyright notice, links to a privacy policy or contact details. It is normally placed at the bottom of a section or page.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing semantic tags makes code more readable and improves SEO and accessibility. It is recommended to apply them whenever you can, instead of faceless `<div>` elements, in order to mark every block\'s purpose clearly.","<body>\\n  <header>\\n    <h1>My blog</h1>\\n    <nav>...</nav>\\n  </header>\\n  <main>\\n    <article>\\n      <header>\\n        <h2>The article\'s title</h2>\\n        <time>2025-01-01</time>\\n      </header>\\n      <section>\\n        <h3>Introduction</h3>\\n        <p>The text of the introduction...</p>\\n      </section>\\n      <section>\\n        <h3>The main part</h3>\\n        <p>The details...</p>\\n      </section>\\n      <footer>\\n        <p>Author: Ivan</p>\\n      </footer>\\n    </article>\\n  </main>\\n  <footer>\\n    <p>&copy; 2025 The blog</p>\\n  </footer>\\n</body>"],[212,"Hidden elements can be perceived differently by screen readers. Using the CSS property `display: none` or the `hidden` attribute removes an element from the accessibility tree entirely, and the screen reader ignores it. The property `visibility: hidden` also hides an element both visually and from the screen reader. If an element is hidden with `opacity: 0` or `clip`, however, it may remain available to the screen reader, which can confuse the user. To hide something from screen readers semantically, the attribute `aria-hidden=\\"true\\"` is used.","The effect of hidden elements on screen readers\\nScreen readers rely on the accessibility tree, which the browser builds from the HTML code. The way an element is hidden directly affects whether it is included in that tree and therefore announced to the user.\\nThe main ways of hiding and their effect\\n\\n- display: none and the hidden attribute: they remove the element from the accessibility tree entirely. The screen reader does not see or announce it. This is the right way to hide content that should not be available to anyone.\\n- visibility: hidden: likewise, the element is hidden both from the visual presentation and from the accessibility tree.\\n- opacity: 0 and clip: the element is visually invisible but remains in the accessibility tree. The screen reader may read it out, which can disorient the user, since they cannot see the element on the screen.\\n- aria-hidden=\\"true\\": this attribute tells the screen reader explicitly to ignore the element, regardless of its visual state. It does not remove the element from the DOM but excludes it from the accessibility tree.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo ensure accessibility it is important to choose the right way of hiding elements. Use `display: none` or `hidden` for content that should not be available to anyone. Apply `aria-hidden=\\"true\\"` for decorative elements or content that is visually present but carries no meaning for screen-reader users. Avoid using `opacity: 0` to hide content that should not be announced.","<!-- The element is hidden from everyone completely -->\\n<div style=\\"display: none;\\">Nobody will see or hear this text</div>\\n\\n<!-- The element is visually hidden but available to a screen reader (bad practice) -->\\n<div style=\\"opacity: 0;\\">This text will be heard but not seen</div>\\n\\n<!-- The element is visually visible but hidden from the screen reader -->\\n<div aria-hidden=\\"true\\">This text will be seen but not heard</div>"],[213,"When the HTML parser meets a `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, it stops building the DOM, loads the script\'s file and executes it, and only then resumes parsing. This is why such a script is called blocking: the content after it is not processed until it has finished running.","The blocking behaviour of the script tag\\nWhen the browser parses HTML and meets a `<script>` tag, it has to assume that the script may change the document — through `document.write`, for example — and therefore cannot carry on building the DOM in parallel. The parsing is paused, the script is loaded (if an external `src` is given) and executed, and only after that does the parser carry on from the place where it stopped. That is why a script in the `<head>` without attributes delays the appearance of the content.\\n\\nThe `async` and `defer` attributes change this behaviour: both let the file be downloaded in parallel with the parsing. `async` executes the script as soon as it has loaded, without guaranteeing the order; `defer` postpones execution until the HTML has been parsed fully, preserving the order of declaration.\\n\\nConclusion: for the main code that works with the DOM, use `defer`, and `async` for independent scripts such as analytics, so that the `<script>` tag does not block the building of the DOM.",null],[214,"Gaps appear between inline-block elements because the browser interprets the spaces and line breaks in the HTML code as text nodes. Those spaces are displayed as ordinary spaces between the elements, creating visible gaps. To remove them you can use font-size: 0 on the parent, comments between the tags, or write the elements on one line.","The cause of the gaps\\nWhen you use display: inline-block on elements, the browser treats them as inline blocks. In HTML the spaces, tabs and line breaks between tags are counted as text nodes. Since inline-block elements behave like text, those spaces are displayed as visible gaps.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nA gap about 4px wide will appear between the red and the blue square.\\nWays of solving it\\n\\n- Remove the spaces from the markup: write the elements on one line without spaces.\\n- Use comments: <!-- --> between the tags.\\n- font-size: 0: set font-size: 0 on the parent and then state the font size explicitly for the child elements.\\n- A negative margin: apply margin-right: -4px (it depends on the font size).\\nAn example with font-size: 0\\n\\nThis method is often used in practice, since it requires no change to the HTML structure.\\nConclusion\\nGaps between inline-block elements are a consequence of how HTML handles whitespace. Understanding this helps you avoid unexpected gaps in your markup. It is recommended to use font-size: 0 on the parent or to write the elements on one line, for the sake of clean code.","<div>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red;\\"></span>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue;\\"></span>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div style=\\"font-size: 0;\\">\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red; font-size: 16px;\\"></span>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue; font-size: 16px;\\"></span>\\n</div>"],[215,"Inline styles are styles set directly in the style attribute of an HTML element. They have a high priority, but their use is justified only in rare cases — for dynamic styles that depend on JavaScript, for example, or for quick prototyping. In production it is better to use CSS classes, since they improve readability, reusability and performance.","What inline styles are and when they are applied\\nInline styles are a way of setting CSS properties directly in the `style` attribute of an HTML element. For example: `<div style=\\"color: red; font-size: 16px;\\">Text</div>`. This approach lets you apply styles to a particular element quickly without creating separate CSS rules. Its use has both advantages and drawbacks, however.\\nThe pros and cons of inline styles\\n\\n- Pros: a high priority (it overrides most other styles), convenience for dynamic styles (when changed through JavaScript, for example), simplicity for quick testing.\\n- Cons: poor reusability (the styles are tied to a particular element), difficulty of maintenance (changes require every element to be edited), an increase in the size of the HTML, the impossibility of using pseudo-classes and media queries, reduced performance when they are changed frequently through the DOM.\\nExamples of use\\nHere is an example where inline styles may be justified — changing a colour dynamically depending on state:\\n\\nFor static styling, however, it is better to use classes:\\n\\nConclusion\\nInline styles are worth applying only for dynamic changes that cannot be implemented through CSS classes, or in extremely small projects. In most cases external CSS files or CSS modules are preferable, for better maintainability and performance.","const button = document.getElementById(\'myButton\');\\nbutton.style.backgroundColor = isActive ? \'green\' : \'red\';\\n\\n<style>\\n  .active { background-color: green; }\\n  .inactive { background-color: red; }\\n</style>\\n<div class=\\"active\\">A button</div>"],[216,"On Android the browser used most often is Google Chrome. It is pre-installed on most devices and is tightly integrated with Google\'s services. Other popular browsers include Samsung Internet, Firefox and Opera.","Popular browsers on Android\\nOn the Android mobile platform the most widespread browser is Google Chrome. This is because Chrome is pre-installed on most Android devices, especially those certified by Google. It provides deep integration with Google\'s ecosystem and synchronizes bookmarks, passwords and history between devices.\\nOther popular browsers\\n\\n- Samsung Internet — pre-installed on Samsung devices; it has a convenient interface and supports extensions.\\n- Mozilla Firefox — it offers extended privacy settings and support for add-ons.\\n- Opera — known for its built-in VPN and its data-saving mode.\\nConclusion\\nGoogle Chrome dominates on Android thanks to being pre-installed and integrated with Google\'s services, but other browsers also have their audience thanks to their unique features.",null],[217,"On the desktop the browser used most often is Google Chrome. Its market share is around 65-70 %. Other popular browsers are Safari, Firefox and Edge. The statistics can change over time.","Which browser is used most often on the desktop?\\nOn desktop computers the most popular browser is Google Chrome. According to various analytics services its market share is consistently around 65-70 %. This means that roughly two out of every three PC users open web pages in Chrome.\\nWhy does Chrome lead?\\n\\n- Speed and performance: Chrome is known for loading pages quickly and handling JavaScript efficiently.\\n- Extensions: the huge library of extensions in the Chrome Web Store lets you configure the browser for any task.\\n- Integration with Google\'s services: convenient synchronization with Gmail, Google Drive, YouTube and other services.\\n- Security: regular updates and built-in mechanisms for protection against malicious sites.\\nOther popular browsers\\n\\n- Safari: it takes second place and is especially popular among macOS users.\\n- Firefox: valued for its privacy and its open source code.\\n- Microsoft Edge: it is gaining popularity thanks to its integration with Windows and its use of the Chromium engine.\\nConclusion\\nWhen developing web applications it is important to test them in Google Chrome first and foremost, since that will ensure they work correctly for most users. You should not forget the other browsers either, however, in order to guarantee compatibility with different platforms.",null],[218,"Yes, older device models and browsers have to be taken into account if your target audience uses them. This ensures the application is accessible and works correctly for every user. Apply progressive enhancement or graceful degradation, so that the basic functionality works everywhere while modern capabilities are added for newer browsers.","Why take older devices into account?\\nSupporting older device models and browsers matters for making your web application accessible. If a significant part of your target audience uses obsolete browsers (Internet Explorer 11 or old versions of Safari on iOS, for example), ignoring them can lead to losing users and to lower conversion.\\nApproaches to support\\n\\n- Progressive enhancement — a basic version that works on every device is built first, and then modern features are added for newer browsers.\\n- Graceful degradation — the full version for modern browsers is developed first, and then it is made to work properly (possibly with a simplified interface) on older ones.\\nA code example: checking support for CSS Grid\\n\\nIn this example modern browsers use Grid while older ones fall back to Flexbox.\\nConclusion\\nTaking older devices into account is necessary if your audience uses them actively. Apply progressive enhancement to ensure the basic functionality on every platform, and add modern capabilities as an enhancement for newer browsers.","@supports (display: grid) {\\n  .container {\\n    display: grid;\\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/* A fallback for older browsers */\\n.container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\\n}\\n\\n.container > * {\\n  flex: 1 1 50%;\\n}"],[219,"Open the developer tools (F12), go to the Elements tab and select the element. In the Styles panel find the CSS property color or background-color. Click the little coloured square next to the value in order to open the palette and see the exact colour.","Checking a colour through the browser\'s tools\\nThe browser\'s developer tools provide a convenient way of inspecting the colours used on a web page. This is necessary for precise markup, for debugging styles and for ensuring conformity with the design.\\nThe main steps\\n\\n- Open the developer tools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I).\\n- Go to the Elements (or Inspector) tab.\\n- Select the element with the picker tool (the cursor icon) or by clicking the HTML code.\\n- In the right-hand panel find the Styles (or Computed) tab.\\n- Find the CSS property that sets the colour (color, background-color, border-color, for example).\\n- Next to the colour\'s value (#ff0000, for example) there will be a little coloured square — click it.\\nWorking with the palette\\nOnce clicked, a colour palette opens where you can:\\n\\n- See the colour\'s exact value in HEX, RGB, HSL or other formats.\\n- Change the colour by dragging the sliders or typing values.\\n- Copy the colour\'s value to the clipboard.\\nA code example\\n\\nIn the developer tools you will see these colours and be able to check them.\\nConclusion\\nUse the developer tools to check and adjust colours quickly in real time, which is especially useful when writing markup and debugging styles.","<div style=\\"color: #3498db; background-color: #f1c40f;\\">Some example text</div>"],[220,"A HEX colour is a hexadecimal representation of a colour, used in web design. It consists of a hash symbol (#) and six hexadecimal digits that define the intensity of the red, green and blue channels. #FF0000, for example, means red. HEX colours are widely applied in CSS to set the colours of elements.","What is a HEX colour?\\nA HEX colour is a way of representing a colour in the hexadecimal number system, widely used in web development and design. It is based on the RGB model, in which every colour is defined by three channels: red, green and blue. Each channel is encoded by two hexadecimal digits from 00 to FF, which corresponds to the decimal values from 0 to 255.\\nThe structure of a HEX colour\\nA HEX colour begins with a hash symbol (#), followed by six characters: the first two are the red channel, the next two the green and the last two the blue. #FF0000, for example, means the maximum intensity of red (255) and no green or blue — that is, pure red.\\nExamples and use\\nHEX colours are used in CSS to set the colours of backgrounds, text, borders and other elements. Here is an example:\\n\\nThere are also shortened three-character HEX codes — #F00, for example, is equivalent to #FF0000. This is convenient for simple colours.\\nConclusion\\nHEX colours are the standard, convenient way of specifying colours in web design, especially in CSS. They provide precise control over colour and are easy to read, which makes them indispensable to interface developers.","/* A red background */\\nbackground-color: #FF0000;\\n\\n/* Green text */\\ncolor: #00FF00;\\n\\n/* A blue border */\\nborder: 2px solid #0000FF;"],[221,"On iOS every browser, Chrome and Firefox included, is obliged to use Apple\'s WebKit engine. This means that the default and the only full-featured browser is Safari, since the others are merely shells over it. The user can install alternatives, but they have no rendering engines of their own.","Which browser is used by default on iOS?\\nOn iOS devices (iPhone, iPad) the default browser is Safari. It is a pre-installed application from Apple that uses the WebKit engine. It is important to understand that because of Apple\'s policy every third-party browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, for example) is also obliged to use WebKit rather than its own engine (Blink or Gecko). So technically every browser on iOS is a shell over Safari with a different interface and synchronization.\\nWhy does this matter?\\nThis restriction affects web development: if you test a site in Chrome on iOS, it renders the same way as in Safari. To check compatibility it is therefore enough to target Safari and WebKit. The user can change their default browser in the settings, but that will not change the engine.\\nExample code for detecting the browser on iOS\\n\\nConclusion\\nKnowing about this particular helps you avoid errors in cross-browser testing and in optimizing for Apple\'s mobile devices. Always check your sites in Safari on iOS, since it is the only browser with full access to the platform\'s features.","// Checking whether the browser is Safari on iOS\\nconst isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);\\nconst isSafari = /^((?!chrome|android).)*safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent);\\nif (isIOS && isSafari) {\\n  console.log(\'This is Safari on iOS\');\\n} else if (isIOS) {\\n  console.log(\'A different browser on iOS (but it uses WebKit)\');\\n}"],[222,"Browsers can run on the same engine. Google Chrome, Opera, Brave, Microsoft Edge (from 2020) and Vivaldi, for example, use the Blink engine (a fork of WebKit). Safari uses WebKit. Firefox uses Gecko. This matters for cross-browser testing.","What is a browser engine?\\nA browser engine (a rendering engine) is the key component of a browser, responsible for parsing HTML and CSS and displaying a web page on the screen. Different browsers may use the same engine, which means their rendering behaves similarly, but it does not guarantee complete identity because of differences in the other components (the JavaScript engine, the security settings and so on).\\nThe main engines and the browsers based on them\\n\\n- Blink (Google\'s fork of WebKit): Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge (Chromium-based), Opera, Brave, Vivaldi, Samsung Internet.\\n- WebKit: Apple Safari, and every browser on iOS (because of Apple\'s requirements).\\n- Gecko: Mozilla Firefox, Tor Browser.\\n- Goanna (a fork of Gecko): Pale Moon, Basilisk.\\nExample code for checking the engine\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\nKnowing the engine helps you predict how a page will be displayed in different browsers. CSS properties supported in Blink, for example, may not work in Gecko. This is critical when developing cross-browser applications.\\nConclusion: understanding which engines are shared simplifies the testing and optimization of web applications, letting you concentrate on the key differences between the main engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko).","// Detecting the browser engine through navigator.userAgent\\nfunction getEngine() {\\n  const ua = navigator.userAgent;\\n  if (ua.includes(\'Gecko\') && !ua.includes(\'WebKit\')) return \'Gecko\';\\n  if (ua.includes(\'AppleWebKit\') && !ua.includes(\'Chrome\')) return \'WebKit\';\\n  if (ua.includes(\'AppleWebKit\') && ua.includes(\'Chrome\')) return \'Blink\';\\n  return \'Unknown\';\\n}\\nconsole.log(getEngine());"],[223,"The viewport is the visible area of a web page on a device\'s screen. Without configuring it properly, mobile browsers may scale the page, making it awkward to use. The `<meta name=\\"viewport\\">` meta tag is used to control it. It is the foundation of responsive markup.","What is the viewport?\\nThe viewport is the rectangular area of the screen in which a web page is displayed. On a desktop it coincides with the browser window, while on mobile devices it may be smaller than the physical screen. Without an explicit viewport declaration, browsers on phones often try to show the whole page at once, scaling it down, which makes the text and the elements too small.\\nWhy control the viewport?\\nTo create responsive sites that display correctly on any device, the viewport\'s width has to be set equal to the device\'s screen width. This is achieved with a meta tag in the HTML. Without it, users will have to scale the page by hand, which degrades their experience.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe viewport meta tag tells the browser how to control the page\'s dimensions and scaling. The most widespread variant is:\\n\\nThe parameter `width=device-width` sets the viewport\'s width equal to the device\'s screen width. `initial-scale=1.0` sets the initial scale to 100 % (no zoom).\\nAn example of use\\nConsider a simple page without the viewport and with it:\\n\\nWithout the tag the text will be small on a phone and the user will have to zoom in. With the tag the text is displayed at its natural size.\\nConclusion\\nThe viewport is a critically important concept for responsive web design. Configuring it properly is mandatory for any site aimed at mobile devices, and it is the first step towards creating a convenient user interface.","<meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <!-- Without this tag a mobile browser will scale the page down -->\\n  <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>Text that will be readable on a phone.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[224,"Deviations from the mockup are recorded through visual comparison: a screenshot of the markup is overlaid on the mockup in an overlay mode (in the Pixel Perfect plugin, for example). Visual regression testing tools (Percy, Applitools) are also used, which compare screenshots automatically. It is important to document every discrepancy in a bug tracker with a screenshot and a description.","How to record deviations from the mockup\\nDeviations from the mockup are discrepancies between the implemented interface and the approved design. Recording them is critical for maintaining the product\'s quality and a consistent user experience. The main goal is to uncover discrepancies at the early stages of development, in order to avoid accumulating technical debt.\\nMethods of recording\\n\\n- Manual visual comparison: use browser extensions (Pixel Perfect, PerfectPixel) to overlay a screenshot of the mockup on top of the markup. Adjust the transparency and check the spacing, the font sizes and the colours.\\n- Automated visual regression testing: tools (Percy, Applitools, Chromatic) take screenshots of components and compare them with reference images. When a discrepancy is found, a report is generated with the changes highlighted.\\n- Inspecting the code: use DevTools to check the CSS properties (padding, margin, font-size, color) and compare them with the values from the mockup (Figma, Sketch).\\nExample code for an automatic check\\n\\nThe documentation process\\n\\n- Take a screenshot of the problematic area.\\n- Give the exact location (the URL, the component, the screen resolution).\\n- Describe the expected behaviour (from the mockup) and the actual behaviour.\\n- Attach a link to the mockup in Figma or another tool.\\n- Create a ticket in the bug tracker (Jira, Trello) with a priority.\\nConclusion: recording deviations from the mockup is a mandatory part of the QA process. Manual methods suit spot checks, while automated tools save time in regression testing. Use a combination of approaches for maximum coverage.","// An example of using Percy for visual testing\\nimport PercyScript from \'@percy/script\';\\n\\nPercyScript.run(async (page, percySnapshot) => {\\n  await page.goto(\'http://localhost:3000\');\\n  // We take a snapshot of the page\\n  await percySnapshot(\'Homepage\');\\n  // If there are discrepancies, Percy will mark them in its report\\n});"],[225,"Browser extensions can change the DOM, inject scripts, block resources or intercept requests. This can lead to unexpected behaviour in an application — errors in the JavaScript or changed styles, for example. It is important for developers to allow for this in their testing and to use code isolation.","The effect of browser extensions on web applications\\nBrowser extensions are programs that run in the browser\'s context and can interact with web pages. They can affect an application\'s behaviour in several ways, including changing the DOM, injecting additional scripts, blocking network requests or modifying the CSS. This can cause errors, reduced performance or even security problems.\\nThe main mechanisms of influence\\n\\n- Changing the DOM: extensions can add, remove or change the elements on a page, which can break an application\'s logic, especially if it relies on a particular structure.\\n- Injecting scripts: an extension\'s content scripts run in the page\'s context and can intercept events, change global objects or cause conflicts with the application\'s code.\\n- Blocking resources: some extensions block advertising, trackers or scripts, which can leave necessary dependencies missing.\\n- Changing styles: extensions can override CSS, which affects the interface\'s appearance and behaviour.\\nA code example: how an extension can change a page\\n\\nHow to protect your application\\nDevelopers can minimize the effect of extensions by using code isolation (the Shadow DOM, for example), by checking that the necessary elements are present before using them, and by testing in different environments. It is also important to avoid global variables and to use strict type checks.\\nConclusion: browser extensions can affect an application\'s behaviour unpredictably, so it is important to test it in various conditions and to apply code-isolation practices in order to increase its robustness.","// An extension\'s content script that adds a button to the page\\nconst button = document.createElement(\'button\');\\nbutton.textContent = \'Click me\';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(button);\\n\\n// This can break the application if it expects a particular number of elements"],[226,"Cross-browser testing involves checking by hand in different browsers, using automated tools (Selenium, Playwright, for example), using cloud testing services (BrowserStack, Sauce Labs) and using emulators/simulators. Polyfills and CSS prefixes are also applied to ensure compatibility. It is important to test on real devices and browser versions.","The main ways of cross-browser testing\\nCross-browser testing is necessary in order to check that a web application works the same way in various browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and others) and their versions. It helps you uncover problems with display, functionality and performance.\\nManual testing\\nThe developer or tester opens the application in different browsers by hand and checks its behaviour. This method is simple but laborious when there are a great many combinations.\\nAutomated testing\\nTools are used that run the tests in different browsers automatically. Examples:\\n\\n- Selenium WebDriver — it lets you write tests in various languages (Java, Python, JavaScript) and run them in browsers.\\n- Playwright — a modern tool from Microsoft that supports every major browser and has automatic waiting.\\n- Cypress — oriented towards testing in a real browser, but it supports only Chrome-like browsers.\\nAn example in Playwright:\\n\\nCloud services\\nServices such as BrowserStack, Sauce Labs and LambdaTest provide access to real browsers and devices in the cloud. This lets you test on a great many configurations without installing local environments.\\nEmulators and simulators\\nFor mobile browsers, emulators (Android Studio, for example) or simulators (Xcode for iOS) are used. They help you check responsiveness and touch interaction.\\nPolyfills and CSS prefixes\\nTo support older browsers, polyfills (JavaScript code emulating modern APIs) and vendor prefixes in CSS (-webkit-, -moz-, for example) are applied. This is not a testing method but a way of reducing the number of problems.\\nConclusion\\nCross-browser testing should be combined: use automation for regression checks, cloud services to cover a great many configurations, and manual testing for complex scenarios. This matters especially for public web applications with a wide audience.","const { chromium } = require(\'playwright\');\\n(async () => {\\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\\n  await page.goto(\'https://example.com\');\\n  const title = await page.title();\\n  console.log(title);\\n  await browser.close();\\n})();"],[227,"An HTTP response consists of three main parts: the status line, the headers and the response body. The status line contains the protocol version, the status code and a text description. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type or Cache-Control. The response body contains the data that was requested — an HTML page or JSON, for example.","The structure of an HTTP response\\nAn HTTP response is the server\'s reply to a client\'s request. It consists of three key components: the status line, the headers and the body. Each of them plays its own part in conveying information.\\nThe status line\\nThe response\'s first line contains the HTTP version, a numeric status code and a brief description. For example: `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`. The status code indicates the result of processing the request: 200 is success, 404 means not found, 500 an internal server error.\\nThe headers\\nThe headers carry metadata about the response. They may include the content type (`Content-Type`), the size (`Content-Length`), the caching policy (`Cache-Control`) and others. The headers are separated from the body by a blank line.\\nThe response body\\nThe body contains the actual data the client requested. It may be HTML, JSON, an image or any other resource. Not every response has a body — a response with the code 204 No Content, for example.\\nAn example HTTP response\\n\\nIn this example the status line indicates success, the headers describe the type and size of the data, and the body contains a JSON object.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the structure of an HTTP response is necessary for developing web applications, debugging network requests and configuring servers. It is the foundation of working with REST APIs and web technologies.","HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nContent-Length: 27\\n\\n{\\"message\\": \\"Hello, World!\\"}"],[228,"CSP directives determine which resources may be loaded on a page. The main ones are: default-src (the base rule), script-src (scripts), style-src (styles), img-src (images), connect-src (network requests), font-src (fonts), frame-src (frames) and object-src (plugins). They help prevent XSS and injections.","What are CSP directives?\\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP directives are the rules stated in the Content-Security-Policy HTTP header or in a meta tag. They help prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks and other injections.\\nThe main directives\\n\\n- default-src — sets the base rule for every type of resource when no more specific directive is given.\\n- script-src — defines the permitted sources for JavaScript.\\n- style-src — controls the loading of CSS.\\n- img-src — permits the sources for images.\\n- connect-src — manages network requests (fetch, XMLHttpRequest).\\n- font-src — sets the sources for fonts.\\n- frame-src — defines which sources may be embedded in an iframe.\\n- object-src — controls the loading of plugins (Flash, for example).\\nAn example of use\\n\\nIn this example only the site\'s own resources are permitted, plus scripts from Google APIs, inline styles and images from any source.\\nConclusion\\nCSP directives are a powerful tool for increasing the security of web applications. They are worth applying in any project where protection against XSS and control over the resources that load matter.","Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://apis.google.com; style-src \'self\' \'unsafe-inline\'; img-src *;"],[229,"A hash in CSP is a cryptographic hash (SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512) of the contents of an inline script or style. It is added to the Content-Security-Policy header in order to permit the execution only of inline code whose hash matches. This lets you use inline scripts safely without abandoning CSP.","What is a hash in CSP?\\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that helps prevent XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which resources may be loaded. Inline scripts (code right in the HTML) are hard to control, however, since they have no external source. To solve this problem CSP supports the use of hashes.\\nA hash is a cryptographic hash (SHA-256, for example) of the contents of an inline script or style. You compute the hash of the script\'s exact text and add it to your CSP policy. When the page loads, the browser computes the hash of every inline script and compares it with the permitted ones. If the hash matches, the script runs; otherwise it is blocked.\\nHow does it work in practice?\\nSuppose you have an inline script:\\n\\nYou compute its SHA-256 hash (with an online tool or on the command line, for example). You get a string like \'sha256-abc123...\'. You then add it to the CSP header:\\n\\nNow the browser will permit only that particular script to run. If someone tries to inject a different inline script, its hash will not match and it will be blocked.\\nWhere is this applied?\\nA hash in CSP is useful when you cannot do without inline scripts (for analytics or custom widgets, for example) and want to keep a strict security policy. It is an alternative to using a nonce (a one-off token).\\nConclusion: use a hash in CSP when you need to permit particular inline scripts without compromising security. It is a reliable way of protecting yourself against XSS while keeping your markup flexible.","<script>alert(\'Hello\');<\/script>\\n\\nContent-Security-Policy: script-src \'sha256-abc123...\'"],[230,"Protecting the client side involves preventing XSS by escaping input, using a Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources of scripts, mandatory HTTPS for encrypting traffic, validating data on both the client and the server, and applying the same-origin policy to isolate resources.","The main methods of protecting the client side\\nThe security of an application\'s client side is aimed at protection against attacks such as cross-site scripting (XSS), request forgery (CSRF) and the interception of data. These methods help prevent the theft of sessions, the injection of malicious code and the leaking of confidential information.\\nThe key approaches\\n\\n- Content Security Policy (CSP) — an HTTP header restricting the sources from which scripts, styles and other resources may be loaded. `Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'`, for example, blocks inline scripts.\\n- Escaping input — converting special characters (`<` into `&lt;`, for example) in order to prevent XSS. In React this is done automatically through JSX.\\n- HTTPS — encrypting the data between client and server, protecting against interception (man-in-the-middle).\\n- The same-origin policy — restricting scripts\' access to resources on another domain. CORS is used for cross-domain requests.\\n- Validation and sanitization — checking the data on the client (with regular expressions, for example) and checking it again on the server, which is mandatory.\\nA code example: CSP and escaping\\n\\nConclusion: a combination of CSP, escaping, HTTPS and validation creates multi-layered protection. These methods are mandatory for any web application that works with user data, especially in forms, comments and authentication.","// Setting CSP through a meta tag\\n<meta http-equiv=\\"Content-Security-Policy\\" content=\\"default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://trusted.cdn.com\\">\\n\\n// Escaping input in JavaScript\\nfunction escapeHTML(str) {\\n  return str.replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\')\\n            .replace(/</g, \'&lt;\')\\n            .replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\')\\n            .replace(/\\"/g, \'&quot;\');\\n}\\n\\n// Use in React (automatic)\\nconst userInput = \\"<script>alert(\'xss\')<\/script>\\";\\nreturn <div>{userInput}</div>; // Safe"],[231,"The main client-side vulnerabilities are: XSS (cross-site scripting), CSRF (cross-site request forgery), clickjacking, the insecure storage of data (localStorage, cookies) and leaks through Referer headers. XSS lets a malicious script be injected; CSRF lets an action be performed on the user\'s behalf. The defences: escaping output, a Content Security Policy, SameSite cookies, the X-Frame-Options header.","The main client-side vulnerabilities in frontend work\\nClient-side vulnerabilities are security problems that arise in the user\'s browser. They can lead to the theft of data, the performance of unauthorized actions or the compromise of a session. The most widespread kinds are: XSS, CSRF, clickjacking and the insecure storage of data.\\nXSS (Cross-Site Scripting)\\nXSS lets an attacker inject malicious JavaScript code into a page, which is then executed in the victim\'s browser. There are three kinds: reflected, stored and DOM-based. An example of vulnerable code:\\n\\nThe defences: escaping all data before inserting it into HTML, using a Content Security Policy (CSP), avoiding innerHTML where possible.\\nCSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery)\\nCSRF makes the victim\'s browser send a request to the target site on their behalf, using their cookies. An attacker might place a form on their own site that sends a POST request to change a password, for example. The defences: using CSRF tokens (unique to the session), checking the Origin/Referer header, setting the SameSite attribute on cookies (Strict or Lax).\\nClickjacking\\nClickjacking hides the target site in an invisible iframe while the user thinks they are clicking the elements of another site. The defences: setting the header X-Frame-Options: DENY or SAMEORIGIN, using a Content Security Policy with the frame-ancestors directive.\\nThe insecure storage of data\\nStoring sensitive data (tokens, passwords) in localStorage or sessionStorage makes it reachable by any script on the page. It is recommended to use httpOnly cookies for tokens, and localStorage only for non-critical data.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the client-side vulnerabilities is necessary for writing secure code. Apply escaping, CSP, CSRF tokens and the proper storage of data in order to minimize the risks. These practices are mandatory for any frontend developer who works with user data.","// Vulnerable code: inserting the user\'s input directly into HTML\\ndocument.getElementById(\'output\').innerHTML = userInput;\\n\\n// The safe variant: using textContent\\ndocument.getElementById(\'output\').textContent = userInput;"],[232,"CSP (Content Security Policy) is an HTTP header that lets you control which resources (scripts, styles, images) may be loaded on a page. It prevents XSS attacks by blocking the execution of inline scripts and the loading of resources from untrusted sources. CSP is set through the Content-Security-Policy header and can be configured for different kinds of content.","What CSP is and which threats it addresses\\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism implemented through an HTTP header that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP\'s main purpose is protection against cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks and other kinds of content injection.\\nThe main threats CSP addresses:\\n\\n- XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) — attacks in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a page. CSP blocks the execution of inline scripts and of scripts from untrusted sources.\\n- Clickjacking — attacks in which a user is tricked into clicking a hidden element. CSP can forbid the page from being embedded in an iframe.\\n- Data injection — the injection of malicious data through forms or the URL. CSP restricts the sources from which data may be loaded.\\nHow does CSP work?\\nCSP is set through the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header and contains directives defining the permitted sources for various kinds of content. For example:\\n\\nIn this example:\\n\\n- `default-src \'self\'` — permits every resource to be loaded only from the same domain.\\n- `script-src \'self\' https://trusted.cdn.com` — permits scripts only from your own domain and the named CDN.\\n- `style-src \'self\' \'unsafe-inline\'` — permits inline styles (though this is not recommended for security).\\nAn example of an XSS attack and protection with CSP\\nSuppose a page has a vulnerability that lets a script be inserted:\\n\\nWithout CSP this script would run. With CSP, if the `script-src` directive does not permit inline scripts, the browser will block its execution and show an error in the console.\\nConclusion\\nCSP is a powerful tool for protecting web applications against XSS and other content-injection attacks. It should be applied on every production site, especially those that handle user input or use third-party scripts. Configuring CSP properly requires a balance between security and functionality, but even a basic configuration reduces the risks considerably.","Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://trusted.cdn.com; style-src \'self\' \'unsafe-inline\';\\n\\n<script>alert(\'XSS\')<\/script>"],[233,"HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language for creating web pages. It uses tags to define a document\'s structure and content — headings, paragraphs, links and images. Browsers interpret the HTML and display it as a visual page.","What is HTML?\\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the fundamental markup language used to create and structure the content of web pages. It is not a programming language, since it contains no logic or algorithms; it serves to describe how text, images, links and other elements should be organized and displayed in the browser.\\nThe main concepts\\nHTML works on the basis of tags, which are enclosed in angle brackets. Tags normally come in pairs: an opening one and a closing one — `<p>` and `</p>`, for example. The content sits inside the tags. Tags\' attributes let you set additional properties such as links (`href`) or identifiers (`id`).\\nAn example of a simple HTML page\\n\\nIn this example `<!DOCTYPE html>` declares the document\'s type, `<html>` is the root element, `<head>` contains the metadata (the tab\'s title, for example) and `<body>` the page\'s visible content.\\nWhere HTML is applied\\nHTML is used everywhere in web development, from simple static sites to complex web applications. It is the foundation on which styles (CSS) and interactivity (JavaScript) are laid. The modern internet is unimaginable without HTML.\\nConclusion: HTML is a mandatory tool for every web developer. It lets you create structured, accessible web pages that browsers and search engines can read easily.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n    <title>My first page</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n    <p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>\\n    <a href=\\"https://example.com\\">A link</a>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[234,"Responsive markup is an approach in which a site\'s design adapts to the size of the device\'s screen. CSS media queries are used to change the styles according to the width of the browser window. This lets a site look equally good on desktops, tablets and smartphones. The main goal is to make reading and navigation comfortable without horizontal scrolling.","What is responsive markup?\\nResponsive web design is a method of creating web pages that adapt automatically to the size of the user\'s device screen. Unlike fixed markup, where the width is set in pixels, responsive markup uses relative units (percentages, em, rem) and media queries to change the layout.\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- A flexible grid: elements are positioned as percentages of the parent container\'s width.\\n- Flexible images: pictures scale with max-width: 100%.\\n- Media queries: CSS rules applied under particular conditions (when the screen is narrower than 768px, for example).\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nResponsive markup is used in most modern sites, especially in online shops, blogs and news portals. It is critically important for SEO, since Google gives preference to mobile-friendly sites.\\nConclusion: responsive markup is the standard of modern web design. It is worth applying whenever a site has to be available on different devices, in order to ensure a good user experience and a high position in search results.","/* The basic grid */\\n.container {\\n  width: 100%;\\n  max-width: 1200px;\\n  margin: 0 auto;\\n}\\n\\n/* A media query for tablets */\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .container {\\n    padding: 0 15px;\\n  }\\n  .sidebar {\\n    display: none;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/* A media query for phones */\\n@media (max-width: 480px) {\\n  .header {\\n    font-size: 18px;\\n  }\\n}"],[235,"HTML is the textual markup you write in a file. The DOM (Document Object Model) is the object model of the document that the browser creates from the HTML. The DOM is a living, dynamic tree of nodes that can be changed with JavaScript, whereas the HTML stays unchanged. Changes to the DOM are not saved back into the HTML file.","What are HTML and the DOM?\\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) is a markup language that describes a web page\'s structure as text with tags. When the browser loads an HTML file, it parses it and builds the DOM (Document Object Model) — an object representation of the document in memory. The DOM is a tree structure in which every HTML tag becomes a node and the text inside the tags becomes text nodes.\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- Static vs dynamic: HTML is static — it is just text. The DOM is dynamic; it can be changed through JavaScript (adding, removing and changing nodes).\\n- The representation: HTML is a string. The DOM is an object model you can interact with programmatically.\\n- The effect on the page: changes to the DOM are reflected on the page immediately (the browser repaints it). Changes to the HTML file require the page to be reloaded.\\nAn example\\nImagine you have this HTML:\\n\\nAfter parsing it, the browser creates a DOM node for the `<div>` and a text node \\"Hello\\". With JavaScript you can change the text:\\n\\nThe DOM has now changed and the page displays \\"World\\", but the original HTML file has stayed the same.\\nConclusion\\nThe DOM is the bridge between static HTML and dynamic JavaScript. Understanding this difference is necessary for working with interactive web pages where the content has to change without a reload.","<div id=\\"app\\">Hello</div>\\n\\nconst div = document.getElementById(\'app\');\\ndiv.textContent = \'World\';"],[236,"The method document.getElementById(\'id\') returns the element with the given id. If the element is not found, it returns null. This is the fastest way of finding a single element by its unique identifier.","The basics of the getElementById method\\nThe `document.getElementById()` method is one of the simplest and fastest ways of reaching a DOM element in JavaScript. It takes a string — the value of an element\'s `id` attribute — and returns the element itself if it exists, or `null` if the element is not found.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis method is used in any web application where you need to get hold of a particular element quickly in order to change its content or styles or handle its events. It is especially useful when working with forms, modal windows and dynamic interfaces.\\nConclusion\\nUse `getElementById` for quick access to a page\'s unique elements, when the id is known and guaranteed to be unique.","<div id=\\"header\\">A heading</div>\\n<script>\\n  const header = document.getElementById(\'header\');\\n  if (header) {\\n    header.style.color = \'blue\';\\n  }\\n<\/script>"],[237,"The simplest way is to assign an empty string to the innerHTML property: element.innerHTML = \'\'. This is inefficient, however, and can cause memory leaks. It is better to use element.textContent = \'\' or a loop with removeChild. The modern method is element.replaceChildren(), which removes every child node in a single call.","Clearing the contents of a DOM element\\nClearing a DOM element is a frequent task when working with a dynamic interface. The wrong approach can lead to memory leaks or slow performance. Let us look at the main methods.\\nThe methods of clearing\\n\\n- innerHTML = \'\' — the simplest, but slow and potentially dangerous: it removes every child element, event handlers included, which can cause leaks.\\n- textContent = \'\' — faster than innerHTML, since it does not parse HTML. It removes only the text nodes and does not touch the child elements.\\n- removeChild in a loop — a reliable way; it removes every child node one at a time. For example:\\n\\n- replaceChildren() — the modern method (ES2021); it removes every child node in a single call. For example:\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor most cases use `replaceChildren()` — it is fast, safe and readable. If support for older browsers is needed, a loop with `removeChild` will do. Avoid `innerHTML = \'\'` because of the risk of leaks and its performance.","const parent = document.getElementById(\'container\');\\nwhile (parent.firstChild) {\\n  parent.removeChild(parent.firstChild);\\n}\\n\\nconst parent = document.getElementById(\'container\');\\nparent.replaceChildren();"],[238,"The loading of fonts can block the rendering of text, causing delays. Browsers use different strategies: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text), where the text is invisible until the font has loaded, or FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text), where the text is displayed in a fallback font. This affects the perceived loading speed and can degrade the user experience.","The effect of font loading on rendering\\nWhen a web page uses custom fonts, the browser has to load them before it can display the text. This process can delay rendering and affect performance. There are two main behaviours: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text) and FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text).\\nFOIT and FOUT\\n\\n- FOIT: the browser hides the text until the font has loaded. This can leave empty space on the page.\\n- FOUT: the browser displays the text in a fallback font first and replaces it with the custom one once it has loaded. This causes a visible change of style.\\nHow to optimize the loading of fonts\\nUse `font-display` in your CSS to control the behaviour. For example:\\n\\nThe value `swap` enables FOUT, which improves the perceived speed. You can also preload fonts with `<link rel=\\"preload\\">`.\\nConclusion\\nConfiguring font loading properly is critical for performance and UX. Use `font-display: swap` and preloading in order to minimize delays and avoid invisible text.","@font-face {\\n  font-family: \'MyFont\';\\n  src: url(\'myfont.woff2\') format(\'woff2\');\\n  font-display: swap; /* Uses FOUT */\\n}"],[239,"The onload and onerror properties are event handlers called when a resource (an image or a script, for example) loads or fails to load. onload fires when the resource has loaded successfully, while onerror fires when an error has occurred (the file was not found, or the network is unavailable, for example). They let you run code once loading has finished or when a problem arises.","The difference between onload and onerror\\nThe `onload` and `onerror` properties are event handlers assigned to HTML elements that load external resources, such as `<img>`, `<script>`, `<link>` and others. They let you respond to the successful loading of a resource or to an error during it.\\nWhen onload fires\\nThe `load` event (the `onload` handler) happens when a resource has loaded fully and is ready for use. For an image, for example, this means it has been downloaded completely and can be displayed; for a script, that it has been executed.\\nWhen onerror fires\\nThe `error` event (the `onerror` handler) arises if an error occurred while loading the resource. The causes may vary: an incorrect URL, a missing file on the server, network problems, CORS blocking and so on. It is important to note that `onerror` does not fire on syntax errors in a script — only on loading errors.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `onload` to perform actions once a resource has loaded successfully, and `onerror` to handle errors and provide fault tolerance (substituting a fallback image, for example). These handlers are especially useful when loading resources dynamically and in situations where correct display of the content has to be guaranteed.","const img = new Image();\\nimg.onload = function() {\\n  console.log(\'The image loaded successfully\');\\n  document.body.appendChild(img);\\n};\\nimg.onerror = function() {\\n  console.error(\'Error loading the image\');\\n  // Show a fallback image\\n  img.src = \'fallback.png\';\\n};\\nimg.src = \'photo.jpg\';"],[240,"Images do not block the building of the DOM and the CSSOM, since they are loaded asynchronously. The browser carries on parsing the HTML and building the DOM without waiting for the images to load. This lets a page display its content faster, while the images load later without delaying interactivity.","How the browser handles images\\nWhen the browser meets an `<img>` tag in the HTML, it does not stop parsing the document. Instead it sends a request to load the image asynchronously and carries on building the DOM. This is the key difference from blocking resources such as synchronous scripts or CSS files, which can delay rendering.\\nThe role of the preload scanner\\nModern browsers use a preload scanner that scans the HTML even before the main parser has built the DOM. It finds links to images, scripts and styles and starts loading them in advance. This speeds up the page\'s overall loading, since the requests are sent in parallel with the parsing.\\nThe effect on the page\'s events\\nImages do not block the `DOMContentLoaded` event, which fires once the DOM has been built fully. They can delay the `load` event, however, which waits for every resource to load, images included. This matters for analytics and interactivity.\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example the heading will appear at once while the image loads asynchronously. The `DOMContentLoaded` event will fire before the image has finished loading.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the asynchronous loading of images helps you optimize a page\'s performance, especially for content sites. Use the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute to defer the loading of off-screen images and speed the initial render up.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <title>An example</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <h1>The content is displayed at once</h1>\\n  <img src=\\"large-image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A large image\\">\\n  <script>\\n    document.addEventListener(\'DOMContentLoaded\', () => {\\n      console.log(\'The DOM is ready; the image may still be loading\');\\n    });\\n  <\/script>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[241,"A graph is a mathematical structure consisting of nodes and edges, used to model relationships. A data chart is a visual representation of numerical data — a line chart or a bar chart, for example. A graph describes relationships, while a chart shows trends and distributions.","The main difference\\nA graph in computer science and mathematics is an abstract data structure consisting of vertices (nodes) and edges (the links between them). It is used to model complex relationships such as social networks, routes or dependencies. A data chart (or plot) is a visual element that displays numerical data on coordinate axes — for analysing trends or comparing values, for example.\\nExamples and uses\\nGraphs are applied in pathfinding algorithms (Dijkstra, for example), in databases (graph databases) and in network models. Data charts are used in analytics, in BI systems and in reports to present statistics clearly.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nA graph is a structure for modelling relationships, while a chart is a tool for visualizing data. The choice depends on the task: graphs suit the analysis of relationships, charts the presentation of numerical series.","// An example of a graph (JavaScript)\\nconst graph = {\\n  A: [\'B\', \'C\'],\\n  B: [\'A\', \'D\'],\\n  C: [\'A\'],\\n  D: [\'B\']\\n};\\n\\n// An example of a data chart (Python with matplotlib)\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]\\ny = [10, 20, 15, 25]\\nplt.plot(x, y)\\nplt.show()"],[242,"Cytoscape is a JavaScript library for visualizing and analysing graphs. It lets you display complex network structures such as social connections or routes. The library supports interactive capabilities: zooming, dragging nodes and updating the data dynamically. Cytoscape.js is widely used in bioinformatics, data analysis and web development.","What is Cytoscape?\\nCytoscape.js is a powerful open-source JavaScript library for visualizing and analysing graphs (networks). It lets developers create interactive web applications that work with data represented as nodes and links. The library provides flexible capabilities for styling, layout and interacting with graphs.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Support for various layout algorithms (circular, hierarchical, force-directed, for example).\\n- Interactivity: selecting nodes, zooming, dragging.\\n- Updating a graph dynamically: adding and removing nodes and edges in real time.\\n- Extensibility through plugins and custom styles.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nCytoscape.js is often used in bioinformatics to visualize molecular interactions, in the analysis of social networks, to display routes in logistics, and in any web application that needs a clear presentation of the relationships between objects.\\nConclusion: Cytoscape.js is a versatile tool for working with graphs in the browser. It is worth applying when you need to visualize complex network structures with the ability to interact with them and analyse them.","// Creating a graph with two nodes and one edge\\nvar cy = cytoscape({\\n  container: document.getElementById(\'cy\'),\\n  elements: [\\n    { data: { id: \'a\' } },\\n    { data: { id: \'b\' } },\\n    { data: { id: \'ab\', source: \'a\', target: \'b\' } }\\n  ],\\n  style: [\\n    { selector: \'node\', style: { \'background-color\': \'#666\', \'label\': \'data(id)\' } },\\n    { selector: \'edge\', style: { \'width\': 3, \'line-color\': \'#ccc\' } }\\n  ],\\n  layout: { name: \'grid\' }\\n});"],[243,"Tokens can be stored in localStorage, sessionStorage or cookies. localStorage and sessionStorage are vulnerable to XSS attacks, since JavaScript can reach them. Cookies with the HttpOnly and Secure flags are safer, since JS cannot reach them and they are transmitted only over HTTPS. For better security it is recommended to use httpOnly cookies and to keep tokens in the application\'s memory.","Ways of storing tokens on the client\\nStoring authentication tokens (a JWT, for example) on the client is a critically important task for the security of web applications. The main ways include using localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies. Each has its own advantages and drawbacks, especially in the context of protection against XSS and CSRF attacks.\\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage\\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage are browser stores reachable through JavaScript. They are simple to use but vulnerable to XSS attacks: if an attacker injects a script, they will be able to read the token. sessionStorage is cleared when the tab closes, which reduces the risk but does not eliminate it entirely.\\n\\nCookies\\nCookies are a safer option if the HttpOnly (unreachable by JavaScript) and Secure (HTTPS only) flags are set. This protects against XSS but requires additional measures against CSRF attacks, such as using the SameSite attribute or CSRF tokens.\\n\\nStoring in memory\\nA token can be kept in a JavaScript variable (in the application\'s memory). This is the safest way, since the token is not stored in the browser, but it is lost when the page reloads and the user has to authenticate again.\\nConclusion\\nFor maximum security it is recommended to use httpOnly cookies with the Secure and SameSite flags. If access to the token from JavaScript is required (to send it in headers, for example), consider keeping it in memory with a short lifetime and using refresh tokens in cookies.","// Saving a token in localStorage\\nlocalStorage.setItem(\'token\', \'my-jwt-token\');\\n\\n// Reading the token\\nconst token = localStorage.getItem(\'token\');\\n\\n// Deleting the token\\nlocalStorage.removeItem(\'token\');\\n\\n// Setting a cookie with flags (on the server)\\nSet-Cookie: token=my-jwt-token; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict"],[244,"D3.js (Data-Driven Documents) is a JavaScript library for creating dynamic, interactive data visualizations in the browser. It lets you bind data to DOM elements and apply transformations to them. D3 provides powerful tools for working with scales, axes and animations. It is not a library of ready-made charts but a tool for building custom visualizations.","What is D3.js?\\nD3.js (Data-Driven Documents) is a JavaScript library for creating dynamic, interactive data visualizations in a web browser. Unlike ready-made solutions such as Chart.js, D3 provides low-level control over the DOM, letting a developer build any kind of chart, diagram or map using HTML, SVG and CSS.\\nThe main concepts\\n\\n- Data binding: D3 lets you bind data to DOM elements. An array of numbers can be bound to a set of SVG circles, for example.\\n- Scales: functions for converting data into visual attributes (coordinates, colours, for example).\\n- Transitions: animations for updating a visualization smoothly when the data changes.\\n- SVG: D3 makes heavy use of SVG for drawing shapes, lines and text.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nD3 is used in analytics, dashboards, scientific publications and any project that needs custom data visualization. It is ideal for creating unique charts that standard libraries do not support.\\nConclusion\\nD3.js is a powerful tool for developers who need complete flexibility in visualizing data. It is worth applying when you need to create non-standard, interactive, animated charts with deep control over every element.","// Creating a simple bar chart with D3\\nconst data = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\\nconst svg = d3.select(\'body\').append(\'svg\')\\n    .attr(\'width\', 400)\\n    .attr(\'height\', 200);\\n\\nsvg.selectAll(\'rect\')\\n    .data(data)\\n    .enter()\\n    .append(\'rect\')\\n    .attr(\'x\', (d, i) => i * 40)\\n    .attr(\'y\', d => 200 - d * 3)\\n    .attr(\'width\', 30)\\n    .attr(\'height\', d => d * 3)\\n    .attr(\'fill\', \'steelblue\');"],[245,"HTTP status codes fall into successful (2xx) and unsuccessful (4xx, 5xx). Successful codes, 200 OK for example, mean the request was processed correctly. Unsuccessful codes signal an error: 4xx is a client error (404 Not Found, for example), 5xx a server error (500 Internal Server Error, for example).","The classification of HTTP status codes\\nHTTP status codes are three-digit numbers that the server sends in response to a client\'s request. They fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit. The main difference between successful and unsuccessful codes is that the successful ones (2xx) indicate the request was processed correctly, while the unsuccessful ones (4xx and 5xx) signal an error.\\nSuccessful codes (2xx)\\nThese codes mean the request was accepted, understood and processed successfully. The most widespread are:\\n\\n- 200 OK — the standard response for successful GET requests.\\n- 201 Created — a resource was created successfully (after a POST request, for example).\\n- 204 No Content — the request was fulfilled but the response body is empty (on a DELETE, for example).\\nUnsuccessful codes (4xx and 5xx)\\nThe 4xx codes indicate an error on the client\'s side, while 5xx indicate an error on the server\'s. Examples:\\n\\n- 400 Bad Request — incorrect request syntax.\\n- 401 Unauthorized — authentication is required.\\n- 403 Forbidden — access is denied.\\n- 404 Not Found — the resource was not found.\\n- 500 Internal Server Error — a general server error.\\n- 502 Bad Gateway — an invalid response from an upstream server.\\n- 503 Service Unavailable — the server is temporarily unavailable.\\nAn example of code handling status codes\\n\\nIn this example `response.ok` returns true for every successful code (2xx). To handle errors we check the specific statuses.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the difference between successful and unsuccessful status codes is necessary for handling the server\'s responses properly, for debugging and for building reliable web applications. Use successful codes to confirm operations and unsuccessful ones to inform the client of errors.","fetch(\'/api/data\')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (response.ok) { // status 200-299\\n      return response.json();\\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\\n      throw new Error(\'The resource was not found\');\\n    } else if (response.status === 500) {\\n      throw new Error(\'A server error\');\\n    } else {\\n      throw new Error(\'An unknown error\');\\n    }\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));"],[246,"When a form is submitted, the browser performs navigation by default to the URL given in the action attribute, sending an HTTP request (GET or POST). This causes the page to reload, since the browser loads a new document from the server. This behaviour is written into the HTML specification for the synchronous submission of data.","Why does submitting an HTML form cause the page to reload?\\nBy default, when the submit button is pressed, an HTML form initiates the browser\'s standard behaviour: it gathers the data from the form\'s fields, forms an HTTP request (GET or POST) and sends it to the server at the URL given in the `action` attribute. Once the response arrives, the browser loads a new document, which is what causes the page to reload. This is a historically established mechanism intended for the synchronous exchange of data between client and server.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the user presses the submit button, the browser performs the following steps:\\n\\n- It gathers the values of every form field (input, select, textarea).\\n- It forms a query string (for GET) or a request body (for POST).\\n- It sends the HTTP request to the server.\\n- It loads the server\'s response as a new page, replacing the current document.\\nAn example of a simple form:\\n\\nOn submission the browser will navigate to the URL `/search?q=value` and load a new page.\\nHow do you prevent the reload?\\nTo create modern single-page applications (SPAs) or to submit data asynchronously, JavaScript is used. The `preventDefault()` method cancels the form\'s standard behaviour, letting you handle the data through AJAX/the Fetch API without a reload.\\nAn example with JavaScript:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding this mechanism matters for developing both traditional multi-page sites and modern SPAs. If you need to avoid a reload, use JavaScript and `preventDefault()`.","<form action=\\"/search\\" method=\\"GET\\">\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" name=\\"q\\" />\\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Search</button>\\n</form>\\n\\ndocument.querySelector(\'form\').addEventListener(\'submit\', function(event) {\\n  event.preventDefault();\\n  const formData = new FormData(this);\\n  fetch(\'/api/submit\', { method: \'POST\', body: formData });\\n});"],[247,"CustomEvent is a constructor built into JavaScript that lets you create events with an arbitrary name and pass additional data with them. It is used to organize the exchange of messages between independent parts of an application — between components or modules, for example. The event is created with new CustomEvent(\'name\', { detail: data }) and then dispatched with element.dispatchEvent(event).","What is a CustomEvent?\\nCustomEvent is a standard browser interface that extends the basic Event and lets a developer create events with an arbitrary name and pass arbitrary data with them through the detail property. It is a powerful tool for implementing the Observer pattern in web applications, when one component wants to notify others of some action without creating tight coupling between them.\\nHow does it work?\\nTo create a custom event the constructor new CustomEvent(type, options) is used, where type is a string with the event\'s name and options is an object that may contain the fields bubbles, cancelable and detail. The detail field is whatever data you want to pass along with the event. The event is then dispatched to the target element with the dispatchEvent() method. Any listener subscribed to that event name on the element or its ancestors (if bubbles: true) will be notified.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhen is this applied?\\nCustomEvent is especially useful in architectures where components have to communicate without depending on one another directly. In micro-frontends, for example, when different parts of an application run in isolated containers, or in simple SPAs without a global state manager. It is also the standard way for web components to report their internal changes to the outside world.\\nConclusion: CustomEvent is a lightweight, native way of implementing loosely coupled communication between the parts of an application. It is worth applying when you need to pass an event between independent modules or components while avoiding direct calls and dependencies, especially in the context of web components or micro-frontends.","// We create a custom event with data\\nconst event = new CustomEvent(\'userLogin\', {\\n  detail: { username: \'john_doe\', timestamp: Date.now() },\\n  bubbles: true,\\n  cancelable: true\\n});\\n\\n// We dispatch the event to the document\\ndocument.dispatchEvent(event);\\n\\n// Somewhere else we subscribe to it\\ndocument.addEventListener(\'userLogin\', (e) => {\\n  console.log(\'The user logged in:\', e.detail.username);\\n});"],[248,"An HTTP request consists of three main parts: the start line, the headers and the body. The start line contains the method, the URI and the protocol version. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type or User-Agent. The body is optional and is used to pass data — in POST requests, for example.","The structure of an HTTP request\\nAn HTTP request is a text message the client sends to the server in order to receive or send data. It consists of three key components: the start line, the headers and the body. Understanding this structure helps developers debug network interaction and configure APIs properly.\\nThe start line\\nThe request\'s first line contains the method (GET, POST, for example), the URI (the path to the resource) and the HTTP version (HTTP/1.1, for example). For example:\\n\\nThe headers\\nThe headers carry additional information about the request, such as the content type, authorization or caching. Every header has the form \\"Key: Value\\". For example:\\n\\nThe request body\\nThe body is not always present and is used to pass data — in POST or PUT requests, for example. It may be in JSON, XML or form-data format. An example body:\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider a complete HTTP request for creating a user:\\n\\nHere the start line is POST /api/users HTTP/1.1, the headers are Host, Content-Type and Content-Length, and the body is a JSON object.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the structure of an HTTP request is necessary for working with web servers, debugging network requests and building client-server applications. It is the foundation for understanding REST APIs and data-transfer protocols.","GET /api/users HTTP/1.1\\n\\nHost: example.com\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nAuthorization: Bearer token123\\n\\n{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"age\\": 30}\\n\\nPOST /api/users HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nContent-Length: 27\\n\\n{\\"name\\": \\"Alice\\", \\"age\\": 25}"],[249,"A request\'s HTTP headers carry additional information about the request, the client or the message body. The main headers are: Host (states the domain), User-Agent (identifies the client), Accept (the response formats), Content-Type (the type of the request body), Authorization (authentication), Cookie (cookies) and Referer (the source of the request). They help the server process the request correctly.","What are a request\'s HTTP headers?\\nA request\'s HTTP headers are fields that the client (a browser, an application) sends to the server along with the request itself. They contain metadata: information about the client, preferences as to the data format, authentication, caching and so on. The headers are sent as key-value pairs, each on its own line, after the request line.\\nThe main request headers\\n\\n- Host — a mandatory header stating the server\'s domain and port (`Host: example.com:8080`, for example).\\n- User-Agent — a string identifying the client application (the browser, its version, the OS).\\n- Accept — states which MIME types of response the client is prepared to accept (`Accept: application/json`, for example).\\n- Content-Type — the type of the content in the request body (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded` or `multipart/form-data`, for example).\\n- Authorization — passes the credentials for authentication (`Bearer <token>`, for example).\\n- Cookie — sends previously stored cookies to the server.\\n- Referer — the address of the page from which the request was made (used for analytics and protection against CSRF).\\nAn example of use in code\\n\\nIn this example the client states explicitly that it is sending JSON, expects JSON in the response and is passing a token for authentication.\\nConclusion\\nA request\'s HTTP headers are necessary for proper communication between client and server: they determine the data format, authentication, caching and other aspects. Knowing the main headers helps when debugging network requests and developing APIs.","fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', {\\n  method: \'POST\',\\n  headers: {\\n    \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\',\\n    \'Authorization\': \'Bearer my-token\',\\n    \'Accept\': \'application/json\'\\n  },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ key: \'value\' })\\n});"],[250,"The browser determines the content\'s type from the Content-Type header in the server\'s HTTP response. That header contains a MIME type — text/html for HTML or application/json for JSON, for example. If the header is absent or incorrect, the browser may try to guess the type (MIME sniffing), but that is unsafe and is often switched off with the header X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff.","How the browser determines the type of content\\nWhen the browser sends a request to the server, the server states the `Content-Type` header in its response. That header contains a MIME type telling the browser how to interpret the data it has received. For an HTML page, for example, `text/html` is used, and for JSON `application/json`.\\nThe role of MIME types\\nMIME types (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) are standardized strings describing a data format. The main types are:\\n\\n- `text/html` — an HTML document\\n- `application/json` — JSON data\\n- `text/plain` — plain text\\n- `application/octet-stream` — binary data\\nAn example of how it works\\nConsider a simple example in Node.js using Express:\\n\\nIn the first case the browser will receive JSON and will most likely offer to download the file or display it as text (depending on its settings). In the second it will render the HTML.\\nMIME sniffing and security\\nIf the `Content-Type` header is absent or stated incorrectly, the browser may try to guess the content\'s type by analysing the response\'s first bytes. This is called MIME sniffing. It can lead to vulnerabilities, however — if an attacker uploads JavaScript disguised as an image, for example. The header `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` is used as protection, forbidding the browser from guessing the type.\\nConclusion\\nSetting the `Content-Type` header properly is critical for the browser to handle the data correctly. Always state the exact MIME type and use `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` for better security.","const express = require(\'express\');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.get(\'/data\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.setHeader(\'Content-Type\', \'application/json\');\\n  res.json({ message: \'Hello\' });\\n});\\n\\napp.get(\'/page\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.setHeader(\'Content-Type\', \'text/html\');\\n  res.send(\'<h1>Hello</h1>\');\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[251,"The browser receives the HTML document and starts parsing it. It breaks the raw HTML into tokens (tags, attributes, text) and then builds nodes from them. The nodes are linked into a tree structure — the DOM (Document Object Model). This process runs in sequence but can be paused when scripts or CSS are met.","How the browser builds the DOM tree\\nWhen the browser loads an HTML document, it begins the parsing process, which turns the raw text into a structured DOM tree. The DOM (Document Object Model) is an object representation of an HTML page that JavaScript can interact with.\\nThe stages of building the DOM\\n\\n- Tokenization: the browser breaks the HTML string into tokens — opening tags, closing tags, attributes and text nodes. `<div class=\\"container\\">`, for example, becomes an opening-tag token with an attribute.\\n- Node construction: node objects are created from the tokens. Every node has a type (Element, Text, Comment and so on) and properties.\\n- Tree construction: the nodes are linked into a hierarchical structure. An opening tag becomes the parent of the nested elements. A closing tag completes the current branch.\\nA code example\\n\\nAfter parsing this HTML the browser will create a DOM tree: the root node `document`, inside it `html`, then `head` with `title`, and `body` with `div` and `p`. Every element becomes an object with methods and properties.\\nThe particulars of the process\\n\\n- Blocking resources: on meeting a `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, the browser pauses the parsing of the HTML, loads and executes the script, and then carries on. CSS can also block rendering, but not the parsing of the DOM.\\n- Incremental processing: the browser does not wait for the whole HTML to load — it starts building the DOM as the data arrives, which speeds up the display of the page.\\n- Errors in the HTML: browsers are tolerant of errors (missing closing tags, for example) and try to recover a correct tree.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the process of building the DOM helps you optimize a page\'s loading: put scripts at the bottom, use the async/defer attributes, and minimize the number of blocking resources. This is the foundation for creating fast, responsive web applications.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <title>An example</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <div id=\\"app\\">\\n    <p>Hello, world!</p>\\n  </div>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[252,"Listening for the scroll event fires very often, causing recalculations and potential lag. This can lead to performance problems, especially on mobile devices. The Intersection Observer API is recommended instead, since it is more efficient and does not cause unnecessary repaints.","The problems with the scroll event\\nThe scroll event fires on every pixel of scrolling, which can mean up to 60-120 calls a second. If the handler performs heavy operations (computing an element\'s position or querying the DOM, for example), this can lead to noticeable stuttering in the interface.\\nThe alternative: Intersection Observer\\nThe Intersection Observer API lets you track an element\'s visibility relative to an ancestor or the viewport asynchronously. It does not block the main thread and fires only when the visibility state changes.\\n\\nWhen to use it\\nIntersection Observer is ideal for infinite scrolling, the lazy loading of images and animations on scroll. It reduces the load on the main thread and simplifies the code.\\nConclusion: to detect reaching the end of a page, use Intersection Observer rather than the scroll event — it improves performance and makes the code easier to maintain.","// The bad approach: the scroll event\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  const scrollTop = window.scrollY;\\n  const docHeight = document.documentElement.scrollHeight;\\n  const winHeight = window.innerHeight;\\n  if (scrollTop + winHeight >= docHeight - 100) {\\n    loadMore();\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// The good approach: Intersection Observer\\nconst sentinel = document.getElementById(\'sentinel\');\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {\\n    loadMore();\\n  }\\n});\\nobserver.observe(sentinel);"],[253,"IntersectionObserver lets you track asynchronously when an element intersects the viewport. For infinite scroll, create an observer that loads new data when it intersects a trigger element and updates the trigger\'s position. This is more efficient than listening for the scroll event, since it works at the browser level without constant computation.","What IntersectionObserver is and why it is needed\\nIntersectionObserver is a browser API that lets you observe asynchronously when a target element intersects its parent or the viewport. In the context of infinite scroll it replaces expensive scroll-event handlers, which are called hundreds of times a second and can slow the interface down.\\nHow infinite scroll works with IntersectionObserver\\nThe core idea is to place an invisible trigger element (a sentinel) at the end of the list. When the user scrolls to it, the observer\'s callback fires and you load the next batch of data. Once it has loaded, the trigger ends up at the bottom again and the process repeats.\\nAn example implementation\\n\\nImportant details\\n\\n- Configure the firing threshold through the `threshold` option (0.1 for early loading, for example).\\n- Use `rootMargin` in order to start loading before the trigger appears on the screen.\\n- Do not forget to disconnect the observer once the data has run out, in order to avoid unnecessary requests.\\nConclusion\\nIntersectionObserver is the modern, performant way of implementing infinite scroll. It suits news feeds, product lists and any dynamic list where smooth scrolling and economy of resources matter.","// HTML: <div id=\\"sentinel\\"></div>\\n\\nconst sentinel = document.getElementById(\'sentinel\');\\nlet page = 1;\\n\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver(async (entries) => {\\n  if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {\\n    const data = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${page}`);\\n    const items = await data.json();\\n    // We add the items to the list\\n    items.forEach(item => {\\n      const div = document.createElement(\'div\');\\n      div.textContent = item.name;\\n      document.body.insertBefore(div, sentinel);\\n    });\\n    page++;\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nobserver.observe(sentinel);"],[254,"During infinite scroll the old DOM nodes have to be removed in order to avoid accumulating elements in memory. Use virtual scrolling, which renders only the visible elements and reuses the nodes. Alternatively you can remove elements outside the visible area by hand — deleting the old ones every time new ones are added, while keeping a buffer, for example.","The problem of accumulating DOM nodes\\nDuring infinite scroll every newly loaded item is added to the DOM. If the old nodes are not removed, the number of elements grows without limit, which increases memory consumption and slows the page down. The browser keeps every DOM node even when they are not visible, which can cause freezes and crashes.\\nThe solution: virtual scrolling\\nVirtual scrolling is a technique in which only a small number of elements visible in the viewport are rendered, plus a small buffer above and below. As the user scrolls, the old elements are removed and new ones added, reusing the DOM nodes. This lets you work with thousands of records without a loss of performance.\\nAn example implementation in JavaScript:\\n\\nIn this example, as the user scrolls, it is computed which elements should be visible and only a small number of them are rendered. The old nodes are removed through `innerHTML = \'\'`.\\nAn alternative approach: manual removal\\nIf virtual scrolling is not suitable, you can remove by hand the elements that are far outside the visible area. When adding new elements, for example, delete the first N old ones, keeping a fixed limit (100 elements, for example).\\n\\nThis approach is simpler but less efficient for very large lists, since the DOM still contains up to 100 nodes.\\nConclusion\\nFor infinite scroll with a large amount of data, use virtual scrolling — it is the standard way of avoiding memory leaks and ensuring smooth operation. For small lists (up to a few hundred items) removing the old nodes by hand will do.","const container = document.getElementById(\'scroll-container\');\\nconst itemHeight = 50;\\nconst buffer = 5;\\nlet items = []; // the array of data\\n\\nfunction render(startIndex) {\\n  container.innerHTML = \'\';\\n  const endIndex = Math.min(startIndex + buffer * 2, items.length);\\n  for (let i = startIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {\\n    const div = document.createElement(\'div\');\\n    div.textContent = items[i];\\n    div.style.height = itemHeight + \'px\';\\n    container.appendChild(div);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ncontainer.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  const scrollTop = container.scrollTop;\\n  const startIndex = Math.floor(scrollTop / itemHeight) - buffer;\\n  render(Math.max(0, startIndex));\\n});\\n\\nconst maxItems = 100;\\nconst list = document.getElementById(\'list\');\\n\\nfunction addItem(text) {\\n  const li = document.createElement(\'li\');\\n  li.textContent = text;\\n  list.appendChild(li);\\n  if (list.children.length > maxItems) {\\n    list.removeChild(list.firstChild);\\n  }\\n}"],[255,"For screen readers to work properly with images, the alt attribute is mandatory. It provides a textual description of the image, which is read out to users with visual impairments. If the image is decorative, alt should be empty (alt=\\"\\") so that the screen reader skips it.","Why is the alt attribute needed?\\nThe alt attribute (alternative text) on an `<img>` tag provides a textual alternative to the image. Screen readers read this text out so that users with visual impairments can understand the image\'s content. Without alt, a screen reader may read out the file\'s name or simply say \\"image\\", which is uninformative.\\nThe rules of use\\n\\n- For informative images: describe the gist of the image briefly but meaningfully. For example: `alt=\\"A chart of sales growth in 2023\\"`.\\n- For decorative images: use an empty alt (`alt=\\"\\"`) so that the screen reader skips the element entirely.\\n- Do not use phrases such as \\"image\\" or \\"picture\\" — the screen reader will announce that it is an image itself.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe alt attribute is mandatory for every image on the web. It makes content accessible to people using screen readers and improves SEO. Always add alt, even if it is empty for decorative elements.","<!-- An informative image -->\\n<img src=\\"chart.png\\" alt=\\"A chart of sales growth by month\\">\\n\\n<!-- A decorative image -->\\n<img src=\\"divider.png\\" alt=\\"\\">\\n\\n<!-- An error: alt is missing -->\\n<img src=\\"photo.jpg\\"> <!-- The screen reader will read out the file\'s name -->"],[256,"Chrome DevTools provides several tabs for profiling: Performance for recording and analysing a page\'s activity, Network for analysing the loading of resources, Memory for investigating memory leaks and taking heap snapshots, and Lighthouse for auditing performance and getting recommendations. These tools help you uncover bottlenecks such as long-running scripts, slow loading or excessive memory consumption.","The main Chrome DevTools tools for profiling performance\\nChrome DevTools is a built-in set of tools that lets developers analyse and optimize the performance of web applications. Several key tabs are used for profiling, each solving its own task.\\nThe Performance tab\\nIt lets you record a session of interaction with the application and then study the timeline of events in detail: the page\'s loading, the execution of JavaScript, rendering, layout and paint. You can see which functions take the most time and find the bottlenecks.\\n\\nThe Network tab\\nIt shows every network request, its size, its loading time and its order. It is useful for uncovering slow requests, a large number of requests or blocking resources. You can filter by type (XHR, JS, CSS) and sort by time.\\nThe Memory tab\\nIt is used to hunt for memory leaks. It lets you take heap snapshots, compare them and find objects that the garbage collector is not freeing. There is also the Allocation instrumentation on timeline tool for tracking memory allocation in real time.\\nThe Lighthouse tab\\nAn automatic audit of performance, accessibility, SEO and best practices. It generates a report with scores and specific recommendations for improvement — optimizing images, reducing the JavaScript bundle or using caching, for example.\\nConclusion\\nThese tools should be applied during development and testing in order to uncover and eliminate performance problems such as slow loading, a stuttering interface or excessive memory consumption, all of which directly affect the user experience.","// An example: recording Performance in order to find long tasks\\n// 1. Open DevTools (F12)\\n// 2. Go to the Performance tab\\n// 3. Press the record button (the round icon)\\n// 4. Perform the actions on the page\\n// 5. Stop the recording and analyse the flame chart"],[257,"The page is repainted after every microtask and macrotask in the current cycle of the event loop has run. The browser schedules rendering before the next macrotask, but it does not guarantee it every cycle. requestAnimationFrame is used to synchronize with rendering.","How the event loop relates to rendering\\nIn the browser the event loop manages the execution of JavaScript, the handling of events and the rendering of the page. Repaints and reflows happen at a special stage that follows the execution of every microtask and macrotask in the current cycle. The browser may combine several changes into a single render in order to optimize performance.\\nThe order of execution\\n\\n- First every macrotask runs (setTimeout, input events, for example).\\n- Then every microtask is processed (Promise.then, MutationObserver).\\n- After that the browser checks whether the rendering needs updating (normally at 60 FPS).\\n- If it does, the rendering stage begins: styles, layout, paint, compositing.\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example requestAnimationFrame runs before the macrotask, since it is tied to the rendering stage.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding this mechanism helps you avoid delays in the UI and use requestAnimationFrame for animations properly, guaranteeing synchronization with the screen\'s refresh.","console.log(\'start\');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\'macro\'), 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\'micro\'));\\n\\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => console.log(\'render\'));\\n\\nconsole.log(\'end\');\\n// The output: start, end, micro, render, macro"],[258,"CSS blocks rendering because the browser does not start painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file. This is necessary in order to avoid displaying the page without styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content). The browser builds the DOM and the CSSOM, and rendering begins only after they have been combined into the render tree. To speed loading up you can use media queries or inline critical styles.","How CSS blocks rendering\\nWhen the browser loads a web page, it goes through the critical rendering path. That path involves several stages: building the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML, building the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS, combining them into the render tree, computing the elements\' geometry (layout) and finally painting.\\nCSS is considered a render-blocking resource because the browser does not start painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file named in a `<link>` in the `<head>`. This is done to prevent the page appearing without styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content, FOUC). If the browser started rendering before the CSS had loaded, the user would first see unstyled content and then a sudden application of styles, which degrades their experience.\\nAn example of the effect on performance\\nImagine you have an external CSS file that takes 2 seconds to load. During those 2 seconds the page will stay blank, even if the HTML has already loaded fully. This increases the time to the first paint and worsens Core Web Vitals metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP).\\n\\nHow to reduce the blocking\\n\\n- Use media queries: if the CSS applies only under particular conditions (for printing, for example), add a `media` attribute. The browser will load such CSS but will not block rendering while the condition is not met.\\n- Inline critical styles: extract the styles needed to display the top of the page (above the fold) and insert them directly into the `<head>` inside a `<style>` tag. Load the rest asynchronously.\\n- Asynchronous loading: use JavaScript to load CSS dynamically after the initial render — with `rel=\\"preload\\"` and the `onload` attribute, for example.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding render-blocking CSS is critically important for optimizing the performance of web pages. By applying techniques such as inline critical styles and the asynchronous loading of non-critical CSS, you can improve loading speed and the user experience considerably, especially on mobile devices and slow connections.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- Blocks rendering -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"print.css\\" media=\\"print\\"> <!-- Does not block rendering on screen -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"non-critical.css\\" as=\\"style\\" onload=\\"this.rel=\'stylesheet\'\\">"],[259,"The `<b>` tag is used to pick text out visually in bold without changing its meaning. The `<strong>` tag denotes the importance of the content, adding semantic weight. For screen readers `<strong>` marks the text out with intonation, while `<b>` is ignored. The difference is in the semantics, not only in the styling.","The semantic difference between `<b>` and `<strong>`\\nIn HTML the `<b>` and `<strong>` tags look identical — the text becomes bold. Their semantic meaning differs, however. The `<b>` tag (bold) is intended for stylistic emphasis that carries no additional importance. The `<strong>` tag indicates that the content is highly important, which affects how screen readers and search engines perceive it.\\nExamples of use\\nUse `<b>` for keywords in a text where no emphasis on importance is needed. Use `<strong>` for warnings or critical messages.\\n\\nThe effect on accessibility\\nScreen readers may change their intonation when reading `<strong>`, underlining its importance. The `<b>` tag causes no such change. This makes `<strong>` preferable for ensuring accessibility.\\nConclusion\\nUse `<b>` for visual emphasis without semantics, and `<strong>` to denote important content. This improves accessibility and the document\'s semantic structure.","<p>Press <b>Submit</b> to finish.</p>\\n<p><strong>Attention!</strong> The data will be deleted.</p>"],[260,"The DOM and the CSSOM are built independently of one another, but they are combined in order to build the render tree. The browser parses the HTML and the CSS in parallel; JavaScript can block the building of the DOM, however, if it is waiting for the CSS to load. The render tree is built only once both trees are ready.","The independent building of the DOM and the CSSOM\\nThe browser processes the HTML and the CSS in parallel, using different threads. The HTML parser builds the DOM (Document Object Model) while the CSS parser builds the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). These processes do not depend on one another and can run at the same time.\\nThe effect of CSS on the DOM through JavaScript\\nAlthough CSS does not block the building of the DOM directly, it can affect it indirectly through JavaScript. If a script tries to reach the styles (through `getComputedStyle`, for example), the browser pauses the execution of the JavaScript until the CSS has loaded and been parsed fully. This can delay the building of the DOM, since scripts block the parsing of the HTML.\\nThe render tree — combining the DOM and the CSSOM\\nOnce the DOM and the CSSOM have been built, the browser creates the render tree, which contains only the visible elements and their computed styles. The render tree is used to compute the geometry (layout) and to paint.\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the script blocks the building of the DOM until the CSS has loaded, even though the CSS itself does not block the DOM.\\nConclusion\\nThe DOM and the CSSOM are built independently, but combining them into the render tree is necessary in order to render the page. Understanding this process helps you optimize a page\'s loading by putting the CSS in the `<head>` and avoiding blocking scripts.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <div>Hello</div>\\n  <script>\\n    // This script will run only once the CSS has loaded\\n    console.log(\'CSS loaded\');\\n  <\/script>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[261,"Content Security Policy (CSP) is an HTTP header that lets you control which resources (scripts, styles, images) may be loaded on a web page. It prevents XSS attacks by blocking the execution of unauthorized JavaScript. CSP is set through directives — script-src \'self\', for example, permits only scripts from the same domain. It is an important layer of security for web applications.","What is a Content Security Policy (CSP)?\\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security standard implemented through the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header. It lets a developer tell the browser which sources of content (scripts, styles, fonts and so on) are considered trusted. CSP protects effectively against cross-site scripting (XSS) and other injections by restricting the execution of unauthorized code.\\nHow does it work?\\nOn receiving the CSP header, the browser checks every resource it loads against the rules that have been set. If a resource does not match the policy, it is blocked. The directive `script-src \'self\'`, for example, permits the execution only of scripts from the same domain as the page. This prevents the execution of inline scripts or scripts from external sites, which may be malicious.\\nAn example of use\\nConsider a simple example of configuring CSP in HTML through a meta tag:\\n\\nHere `default-src \'self\'` sets the base rule — only resources from the current domain are permitted. `script-src` additionally permits scripts from `https://apis.example.com`, while `style-src` allows inline styles (`\'unsafe-inline\'`).\\nWhere is it applied?\\nCSP is used in web applications to strengthen security, especially in systems with user-generated content (forums, blogs, CRMs). It is also mandatory for conformity with security standards such as PCI DSS.\\nConclusion\\nCSP is a powerful tool for preventing XSS attacks and controlling the loading of resources. It is worth applying in any web application where security matters, especially when working with dynamic content or third-party libraries.","<meta http-equiv=\\"Content-Security-Policy\\" content=\\"default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://apis.example.com; style-src \'self\' \'unsafe-inline\';\\">"],[262,"Draft saving for a form lets the user avoid losing the data they have entered if the page is closed by accident or something fails. It is implemented by saving the form\'s state in localStorage or sessionStorage every time a field changes. When the page loads, the data is restored from storage and fills the form in. This improves the user experience and reduces the number of abandoned forms.","Why draft saving for a form is needed\\nDraft saving solves the problem of losing data when a form has not been filled in completely. The user may close a tab by accident, reload the page or run into a network error. Without a saved draft, everything they entered is lost, which causes irritation and lowers conversion. This is especially critical for long forms: registration, placing an order, creating complex objects.\\nHow it works\\nThe core idea is to save the form\'s state in the browser\'s local storage (localStorage) on every change. When the page loads, we check whether any saved data exists and restore it. To do this we subscribe to the fields\' change events (input, change) and periodically write the form\'s current state as a JSON string.\\nAn example implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Registration and login forms\\n- Content editors (blogs, CMSs)\\n- Multi-step forms (wizards)\\n- An online shop\'s basket\\nConclusion: draft saving is a simple, effective way of improving UX, especially for long or critical forms. An implementation through localStorage requires no server-side support and is easy to integrate into any project.","const FORM_KEY = \'draft_form_data\';\\n\\nfunction saveDraft(formData) {\\n  localStorage.setItem(FORM_KEY, JSON.stringify(formData));\\n}\\n\\nfunction loadDraft() {\\n  const data = localStorage.getItem(FORM_KEY);\\n  return data ? JSON.parse(data) : null;\\n}\\n\\nfunction clearDraft() {\\n  localStorage.removeItem(FORM_KEY);\\n}\\n\\n// Saving when the fields change\\ndocument.querySelectorAll(\'input, textarea, select\').forEach(el => {\\n  el.addEventListener(\'input\', () => {\\n    const formData = new FormData(document.getElementById(\'myForm\'));\\n    saveDraft(Object.fromEntries(formData));\\n  });\\n});\\n\\n// Restoring on load\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'DOMContentLoaded\', () => {\\n  const draft = loadDraft();\\n  if (draft) {\\n    Object.entries(draft).forEach(([name, value]) => {\\n      const field = document.querySelector(`[name=\\"${name}\\"]`);\\n      if (field) field.value = value;\\n    });\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// Clearing it after a successful submission\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myForm\').addEventListener(\'submit\', clearDraft);"],[263,"A dropdown list can be clipped because of the CSS property overflow: hidden on the table or on its parent container. The problem also arises if the list\'s element has a low z-index or sits inside a container with a fixed height. The solution is to set overflow: visible on the table, or to use position: fixed for the dropdown list.","The causes of a dropdown list being clipped\\nA dropdown list inside a table is often clipped because of the CSS property `overflow: hidden` applied to the table, to its cell or to a parent container. That property hides any content extending beyond the element\'s boundaries. The problem can also be caused by a low `z-index` value on the dropdown list, or by its sitting inside a container with a fixed height.\\nHow it works\\nTables in HTML have a strict structure, and their cells (`td` or `th`) do not by default let content extend beyond their boundaries unless `overflow: visible` is set. When the dropdown list opens it may extend beyond the cell, but if `overflow: hidden` is set on the table or its parent, part of the list will be hidden.\\nA code example\\n\\nThe solutions\\n\\n- Set `overflow: visible` on the table and its cells.\\n- Use `position: fixed` for the dropdown list, so that it is positioned relative to the browser window.\\n- Place the dropdown list outside the table with JavaScript and absolute positioning.\\nConclusion: the problem of a dropdown list being clipped inside a table is solved by configuring the overflow and positioning CSS properties properly. This matters for creating convenient interfaces, especially in forms and tables of data.","<table style=\\"overflow: hidden;\\">\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>\\n      <div class=\\"dropdown\\">\\n        <button>Open</button>\\n        <div class=\\"dropdown-menu\\">\\n          <!-- the dropdown list -->\\n        </div>\\n      </div>\\n    </td>\\n  </tr>\\n</table>"],[264,"CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is an attack in which an attacker makes the victim\'s browser perform an unwanted action on a trusted site, using its cookies. On the frontend the defence involves using CSRF tokens, which the server generates and checks on every request. Setting the SameSite attribute on cookies (Strict or Lax) and checking the Origin or Referer header also help. It is important not to rely on cookies alone for authentication and to use additional mechanisms such as custom headers.","What is CSRF?\\nCSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is a kind of attack in which an attacker makes an authenticated user\'s browser send a request to the target site without their knowledge. For example, the user visits a malicious site containing a hidden form or image that sends a POST request to a bank\'s server. If the user is already authenticated on that server (the cookies are stored), the request will be performed with their privileges.\\nHow do you protect against it on the frontend?\\nThe main methods of protection on the client side include:\\n\\n- CSRF tokens: the server generates a unique token, which is embedded in the HTML form or passed through a header (X-CSRF-Token, for example). On every request the frontend sends that token, and the server checks it. The token has to be random and tied to the session.\\n- SameSite cookies: setting the attribute SameSite=Strict or Lax on cookies prevents them being sent in cross-site requests. SameSite=Lax, for example, permits them to be sent only for navigational GET requests, which blocks most CSRF attacks.\\n- Checking headers: the server can check the Origin or Referer headers in order to be sure the request came from a trusted source. On the frontend this requires no additional action, but the server has to be configured.\\n- Custom headers: using custom headers (X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest, for example) for AJAX requests, since browsers do not let such headers be set from cross-domain scripts without CORS.\\nAn example implementation of a CSRF token on the frontend\\n\\nIn this example the token is read out of a meta tag that the server inserted into the HTML. It is then added to the request\'s header. The server checks the token and rejects the request if it does not match.\\nConclusion\\nCSRF protection on the frontend is a combination of using CSRF tokens, configuring cookies properly (SameSite) and checking headers. These methods are effective against most attacks but require coordinated work with the server. They are worth applying in any web application that has cookie-based authentication and performs state-changing requests (POST, PUT, DELETE).","// We get the CSRF token out of a meta tag or a cookie\\nconst csrfToken = document.querySelector(\'meta[name=\\"csrf-token\\"]\').getAttribute(\'content\');\\n\\n// We send a POST request with the token in a header\\nfetch(\'/api/transfer\', {\\n  method: \'POST\',\\n  headers: {\\n    \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\',\\n    \'X-CSRF-Token\': csrfToken\\n  },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ amount: 100, to: \'attacker\' })\\n});"],[265,"The method querySelector(\'*\') returns the first element matching the selector \'*\' — that is, any element. To get every element you have to use querySelectorAll(\'*\'), which returns a NodeList with every element on the page. This is useful for iterating over or analysing the whole DOM.","What is querySelector with an asterisk?\\nThe `querySelector` method takes a CSS selector and returns the first element matching it. The selector `*` (an asterisk) in CSS means \\"any element\\". So `document.querySelector(\'*\')` will return the very first element in the DOM (normally `<html>`).\\nHow do you get every element?\\nTo get every element on a page you have to use `querySelectorAll(\'*\')`. This method returns a static NodeList containing every DOM element in the order they appear. For example:\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis can be useful for:\\n\\n- Counting the number of elements on a page.\\n- Applying styles or attributes to every element.\\n- Debugging or analysing the DOM\'s structure.\\nBe careful, though: iterating over every element can be expensive in terms of performance on large pages.\\nConclusion\\nUse `querySelectorAll(\'*\')` when you need a complete list of DOM elements for analysis or bulk operations, but bear the possible performance problems in mind.","const allElements = document.querySelectorAll(\'*\');\\nconsole.log(allElements.length); // the number of all the elements"],[266,"The getElementsByTagName method returns a live HTMLCollection, not a single element. It updates automatically when the DOM changes. To get a particular element you have to index into it or use the item() method.","What does getElementsByTagName return?\\nThe `getElementsByTagName` method returns a live collection of elements — an `HTMLCollection`. This is not a single element but a list of every element in the document with the given tag. The collection is live, which means it updates automatically when elements are added to or removed from the DOM.\\nHow do you work with the result?\\nTo get a particular element, use an index or the `item()` method. For example:\\n\\nIf you need to iterate over every element, use a `for` loop or convert it into an array with `Array.from()`.\\nThe difference from the other methods\\n\\n- `getElementById` — returns a single element (or null).\\n- `querySelector` — returns the first matching element.\\n- `querySelectorAll` — returns a static NodeList collection (not a live one).\\nAn example of use\\n\\nConclusion: `getElementsByTagName` returns a live HTMLCollection, which is convenient for dynamic pages where the DOM changes often. For static lists, however, `querySelectorAll` is better, in order to avoid unexpected changes.","const divs = document.getElementsByTagName(\'div\');\\nconst firstDiv = divs[0]; // the first div\\nconst secondDiv = divs.item(1); // the second div\\n\\n// We get every paragraph\\nconst paragraphs = document.getElementsByTagName(\'p\');\\nconsole.log(paragraphs.length); // the number of paragraphs\\n\\n// We add a new paragraph\\nconst newP = document.createElement(\'p\');\\ndocument.body.appendChild(newP);\\nconsole.log(paragraphs.length); // it will increase automatically"],[267,"The Access-Control-Allow-Origin header states which domains may reach a resource. If the server does not send this header, the browser blocks requests from other origins. The value \'*\' permits every domain but is not supported together with cookies.","What CORS is and why Access-Control-Allow-Origin is needed\\nCORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism that controls access to resources on other domains. When a web page tries to make a request to a server on another domain, the browser checks the headers of the server\'s response. The main header governing the permitted domains is `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.\\nHow it works\\nIn its response the server sends the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header naming the domain that is permitted access. If the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com`, for example, then only pages on that domain can read the response. If every domain has to be permitted, the value `*` is used, but that does not work with requests that carry cookies or authorization.\\nAn example of the configuration on the server (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis header is used in any API called from the frontend on a different domain. If you have a React application on `app.example.com` and an API on `api.example.com`, for example, the API\'s server has to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com`.\\nConclusion: Access-Control-Allow-Origin is the key header for safe cross-domain interaction. Configuring it properly prevents data leaks and keeps modern web applications working.","const express = require(\'express\');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Origin\', \'https://myfrontend.com\');\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Methods\', \'GET, POST\');\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Headers\', \'Content-Type\');\\n  next();\\n});\\n\\napp.get(\'/data\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.json({ message: \'Access permitted\' });\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[268,"Events are normally caught in the bubbling phase. This is the default behaviour for most events, such as click or keydown. The capturing phase is used more rarely, mainly to intercept events before they reach the target element. Bubbling is more intuitive and convenient for event delegation.","The phases of event propagation\\nWhen an event occurs in the DOM, it goes through three phases: capturing, target and bubbling. In the capturing phase the event travels from the root element (document) down to the target element. In the bubbling phase it travels the other way, from the target element up to the root.\\nWhy bubbling is used more often\\nBy default the `addEventListener` method attaches a handler in the bubbling phase (the third argument `false`, or omitted). This is done for convenience: bubbling lets you implement event delegation, where a single handler on a parent handles events from a great many child elements. For a `<ul>` list with a hundred `<li>` items, for example, you can attach one handler to the `<ul>` and catch clicks on any `<li>` through bubbling.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhen capturing is used\\nThe capturing phase is used more rarely — to intercept events before they reach the target element, for example, or for debugging. It is also useful when you need to handle an event before a child element handles it.\\nConclusion: bubbling is the standard and most practical way of handling events in web development, especially for delegation. Capturing is applied in specific scenarios where early interception is required.","// A handler in the bubbling phase (the default)\\ndocument.querySelector(\'ul\').addEventListener(\'click\', (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'A click on\', event.target.tagName);\\n});\\n\\n// A handler in the capturing phase (the third argument true)\\ndocument.querySelector(\'ul\').addEventListener(\'click\', (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'Capturing: a click on\', event.target.tagName);\\n}, true);"],[269,"The main methods for finding elements in the DOM are: getElementById (by id), getElementsByClassName (by class), getElementsByTagName (by tag) and querySelector/querySelectorAll (by CSS selector). The first three return live collections (HTMLCollection), while querySelectorAll returns a static NodeList. querySelector returns the first matching element, querySelectorAll every one of them.","Methods for finding elements in the DOM\\nJavaScript has several ways of finding elements in the DOM tree. The main ones are: `getElementById`, `getElementsByClassName`, `getElementsByTagName`, plus `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll`. They differ in the types they return, in performance and in flexibility.\\ngetElementById\\nIt returns a single element by its unique `id`. This is the fastest method, since the browser optimizes lookups by id.\\n\\ngetElementsByClassName and getElementsByTagName\\nThey return a live `HTMLCollection`, which updates automatically when the DOM changes. `getElementsByClassName` searches by class, `getElementsByTagName` by tag name.\\n\\nquerySelector and querySelectorAll\\nThese methods take a CSS selector. `querySelector` returns the first matching element, `querySelectorAll` a static `NodeList`, which does not update when the DOM changes. They are more flexible but slower than the specialized methods.\\n\\nThe main differences\\n\\n- The type of the return value: `getElementById` returns a single element, `getElementsBy*` a live collection, `querySelectorAll` a static NodeList.\\n- Performance: `getElementById` is the fastest; `querySelector` is slower because the CSS selector has to be parsed.\\n- Flexibility: `querySelector` lets you use any CSS selector, combinators and pseudo-classes included.\\nConclusion: to search by id use `getElementById` — it is fast and simple. For complex selectors, or for searching by class or tag with a live collection, `getElementsBy*` will do. If you need a static collection or a complex CSS selector, choose `querySelectorAll`.","const element = document.getElementById(\'myId\');\\n\\nconst items = document.getElementsByClassName(\'item\');\\nconst divs = document.getElementsByTagName(\'div\');\\n\\nconst firstButton = document.querySelector(\'button.primary\');\\nconst allButtons = document.querySelectorAll(\'button\');"],[270,"For interaction between a page and an iframe the window.postMessage method is used. The parent page sends a message through iframe.contentWindow.postMessage, and the iframe through window.parent.postMessage. On the receiving side the message is caught through the message event. The sender\'s origin must always be checked for security. This is the standard way of exchanging data between windows on different domains.","The basics of interacting with an iframe through postMessage\\nWhen a page and an iframe are on different domains, direct access to one another\'s DOM or functions is blocked by the same-origin policy. The postMessage API built into browsers is used for the safe exchange of data.\\nHow it works\\nA message is sent through the target window\'s postMessage method. The parent reaches the iframe through iframe.contentWindow, and the iframe reaches the parent through window.parent. The second argument is the expected origin, for security.\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis approach is used for widgets, embedded payment forms, support chats and any micro-service interfaces where parts of the UI live on different domains. It is important always to check event.origin in order not to let data leak.\\nConclusion\\npostMessage is the only standard way of cross-domain communication with an iframe. It is safe when the origin is validated properly and suits any scenario that requires the exchange of data between windows from different origins.","// The parent page sends a message into the iframe\\nconst iframe = document.getElementById(\'myIframe\');\\niframe.contentWindow.postMessage({ action: \'update\', data: \'hello\' }, \'https://child-domain.com\');\\n\\n// In the iframe we listen for the message\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'message\', (event) => {\\n  if (event.origin !== \'https://parent-domain.com\') return;\\n  console.log(\'Received:\', event.data);\\n  // We send a reply\\n  event.source.postMessage({ response: \'ok\' }, event.origin);\\n});"],[271,"The `<dialog>` element provides the methods showModal() and close() for controlling its display. In React you have to reach the DOM element through useRef and then call those methods in your event handlers. This lets you create modal windows without third-party libraries.","What the `<dialog>` element is\\nThe `<dialog>` element is a native HTML element intended for creating modal windows and dialogs. It provides the built-in methods showModal() for opening the window in modal mode (with the background blocked) and close() for closing it. This simplifies development, since no additional libraries are required.\\nUse in React through refs\\nIn React the useRef hook is used to reach a DOM element. A ref is created and attached to the `<dialog>` element. The showModal() and close() methods are then called in event handlers (onClick, for example) through the ref\'s current value.\\n\\nParticulars and uses\\nThe showModal() method opens the dialog on top of the rest of the content and blocks interaction with the background. To close it you can use close() or press the Escape key. The `<dialog>` element also supports the \'close\' event, which can be handled for additional logic. This approach is useful for simple modal windows, notifications or forms, when no complex animation or customization is required.\\nConclusion: use `<dialog>` with refs in React to create modal windows quickly without external dependencies, if your project does not require complex state management or styling.","import { useRef } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Modal() {\\n  const dialogRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const openModal = () => {\\n    dialogRef.current?.showModal();\\n  };\\n\\n  const closeModal = () => {\\n    dialogRef.current?.close();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={openModal}>Open Modal</button>\\n      <dialog ref={dialogRef}>\\n        <p>Modal content</p>\\n        <button onClick={closeModal}>Close</button>\\n      </dialog>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[272,"HTTP response codes fall into five groups. 1xx are informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirections, 4xx client errors and 5xx server errors. Each group has its own meaning and helps you understand the result of a request.","The groups of HTTP response codes\\nHTTP response codes are three-digit numbers that the server sends to the client in response to a request. They fall into five groups, each with its own purpose. Understanding these codes helps developers diagnose problems in web applications quickly.\\n1xx: Informational\\nThe codes in this group report that the request has been received and is being processed. They are rarely used in everyday practice but matter for protocols such as WebSocket. An example: `100 Continue` — the client may carry on sending the request body.\\n2xx: Successful\\nThese codes indicate that the request was processed successfully. The best known is `200 OK`, which is returned on a successful GET request. `201 Created` is used after a resource has been created (on a POST, for example). `204 No Content` means success but with no response body.\\n3xx: Redirections\\nThe codes in this group say that additional actions have to be performed in order to fulfil the request. `301 Moved Permanently` means the resource has moved permanently; `302 Found` is a temporary redirect. `304 Not Modified` is used for caching.\\n4xx: Client errors\\nThese codes indicate problems on the client\'s side. `400 Bad Request` — incorrect request syntax. `401 Unauthorized` — authentication is required. `403 Forbidden` — access is denied. `404 Not Found` — the resource was not found. `429 Too Many Requests` — the request limit has been exceeded.\\n5xx: Server errors\\nThe codes in this group signal problems on the server\'s side. `500 Internal Server Error` — a general server error. `502 Bad Gateway` — an invalid response from an upstream server. `503 Service Unavailable` — the server is temporarily unavailable.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nIn this example we handle the different response codes in order to react properly to success or to an error.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the groups of HTTP codes is necessary for debugging network requests, configuring servers and building reliable client-server applications. It is a basic skill for every web developer.","fetch(\'/api/data\')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (response.status === 200) {\\n      return response.json();\\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\\n      console.error(\'Resource not found\');\\n    } else if (response.status === 500) {\\n      console.error(\'Server error\');\\n    }\\n  });"],[273,"IntersectionObserver uses the browser\'s internal mechanism to track asynchronously when an element intersects its parent or the viewport. The browser optimizes the checks by running them within the event loop, normally before rendering. This avoids expensive computation on the main thread and keeps performance smooth.","How IntersectionObserver works under the hood\\nIntersectionObserver is a browser API that lets you track asynchronously when an element intersects its parent container or the viewport. Unlike listening for scroll events by hand, which can cause a great deal of computation on the main thread, IntersectionObserver uses the browser\'s internal optimizations.\\nThe internal mechanism\\nThe browser implements IntersectionObserver at the level of the rendering engine. When you create an observer, the browser registers it in a special data structure linked to the DOM tree. The intersection checks are performed not on every animation frame but within the event loop, normally before the rendering stage. This lets the browser batch the changes and minimize the load.\\nA code example\\n\\nThe optimizations\\n\\n- The browser uses hardware acceleration to determine visibility, without loading the main thread.\\n- The checks are performed only when the scroll position, the window\'s size or the DOM changes, not on every scroll event.\\n- IntersectionObserver supports thresholds, which lets you control precisely when it fires.\\nConclusion\\nIntersectionObserver is an efficient tool for implementing lazy loading, infinite scrolling and animations, since it uses the browser\'s internal mechanisms to minimize the performance cost.","const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      console.log(\'The element is visible\');\\n    }\\n  });\\n}, { threshold: 0.5 });\\n\\nconst target = document.getElementById(\'myElement\');\\nobserver.observe(target);"],[274,"fetch is a method built into the browser for performing HTTP requests. It returns a Promise that resolves to a Response object. A call to response.json() also returns a Promise, which reads the response body and parses it as JSON. This is an asynchronous operation, so you have to use await or .then().","What is fetch?\\nfetch is an API built into the browser for performing HTTP requests. It replaces the obsolete XMLHttpRequest and provides a simpler, more flexible Promise-based interface. fetch takes a URL and an optional options object (the method, the headers, the request body) and returns a Promise that resolves to a Response object.\\nWhat does response.json() return?\\nThe response.json() method is called on a Response object and returns a Promise that resolves to the result of parsing the response body as JSON. This is an asynchronous operation, since the response body may be large and is read as a stream. If the response body is not valid JSON, the Promise is rejected with an error.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nIn this example fetch sends a GET request, then the response\'s status is checked, and if all is well response.json() is called in order to extract the data. Note that response.json() is itself asynchronous, so it has to be handled through .then() or await.\\nConclusion\\nfetch and response.json() are the foundation of working with HTTP in modern web applications. They let you receive and send data in JSON format easily, which is especially useful when interacting with REST APIs. Use them to load data asynchronously on the client.","fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\'Network response was not ok\');\\n    }\\n    return response.json(); // returns a Promise\\n  })\\n  .then(data => {\\n    console.log(data); // we handle the data\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    console.error(\'Fetch error:\', error);\\n  });"],[275,"The main browser storage mechanisms for caching are: localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, IndexedDB and Cache Storage. localStorage stores up to 5-10 MB with no expiry, sessionStorage until the tab is closed, cookies up to 4 KB with an expiry, IndexedDB large volumes of structured data, and Cache Storage HTTP responses. The choice depends on the volume and on how long the data has to live.","An overview of the browser\'s storage mechanisms\\nSeveral mechanisms are available for caching data on the client side in the browser: localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, IndexedDB and Cache Storage. Each has its own characteristics as to volume, lifetime and the way it is accessed, which determines where it is applied.\\nlocalStorage\\nIt stores data as key-value pairs (strings) with a limit of around 5-10 MB. The data is preserved after the browser closes and has no expiry. It suits caching small volumes of static information — a user\'s settings, for example.\\n\\nsessionStorage\\nThe same as localStorage, but the data lives only within the current tab and is deleted when it closes. The limit is also around 5-10 MB. It is used for temporary data — the state of a form on one page, for example.\\n\\nCookies\\nSmall pieces of text data, up to 4 KB, which are sent to the server automatically with every HTTP request. They have an expiry, set through Expires or Max-Age. They are used for authentication and session tracking, but not for caching large volumes.\\n\\nIndexedDB\\nAn object-oriented database in the browser that supports large volumes (hundreds of MB and more). It lets you store structured data, files and Blobs included. Access is asynchronous, through transactions. It is ideal for caching complex data — for offline applications, for example.\\n\\nCache Storage\\nPart of the Service Worker API, intended for caching HTTP responses. It lets you store requests and responses in order to work offline. The limit depends on the browser but is normally substantial. It is used in Progressive Web Apps (PWAs).\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of storage depends on the task: for small string data that has to live a long time use localStorage; for temporary data, sessionStorage; for authentication, cookies; for large volumes of structured data, IndexedDB; for caching HTTP requests in a PWA, Cache Storage. Applying these mechanisms properly improves performance and the user experience.","// Saving in localStorage\\nlocalStorage.setItem(\'theme\', \'dark\');\\n// Reading it\\nconst theme = localStorage.getItem(\'theme\');\\nconsole.log(theme); // \'dark\'\\n\\n// Saving in sessionStorage\\nsessionStorage.setItem(\'formData\', JSON.stringify({name: \'John\'}));\\n// Reading it\\nconst data = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem(\'formData\'));\\n\\n// Setting a cookie\\ndocument.cookie = \'sessionId=abc123; max-age=3600; path=/\';\\n// Reading every cookie\\nconsole.log(document.cookie);\\n\\n// Opening the database\\nconst request = indexedDB.open(\'myDB\', 1);\\nrequest.onsuccess = (event) => {\\n  const db = event.target.result;\\n  const transaction = db.transaction([\'store\'], \'readonly\');\\n  const store = transaction.objectStore(\'store\');\\n  const getRequest = store.get(\'key\');\\n  getRequest.onsuccess = () => console.log(getRequest.result);\\n};\\n\\n// Opening the cache and adding a response\\ncaches.open(\'v1\').then(cache => {\\n  cache.add(\'/api/data\');\\n});\\n// Reading from the cache\\ncaches.match(\'/api/data\').then(response => {\\n  if (response) console.log(\'The data came from the cache\');\\n});"],[276,"By default the `<button>` element has the type \\"submit\\". This means that pressing a button inside a form sends its data to the server. If the button is outside a form, it performs no action. To change the behaviour you have to state the attribute type=\\"button\\" explicitly.","The default type for the `<button>` element\\nIn HTML the `<button>` element has the type `submit` by default. This is an important characteristic that often leads to unexpected behaviour if a developer does not state the `type` attribute explicitly. When a button is inside a form, pressing it causes the form\'s data to be sent to the server. If the button is outside a form, it performs no action.\\nExamples of use\\nConsider a simple form:\\n\\nIn this case the button will submit the form. To prevent the submission you have to state `type=\\"button\\"` explicitly:\\n\\nThere are also the types `reset` (which resets the form) and `submit` (which submits it).\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the default type helps you avoid errors when working with forms. Always state the `type` attribute on buttons in order to control their behaviour.","<form action=\\"/submit\\" method=\\"post\\">\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" name=\\"username\\">\\n  <button>Submit</button>\\n</form>\\n\\n<button type=\\"button\\">Click me</button>"],[277,"Inside `<details>` the `<summary>` tag is used for the heading of the visible part, plus arbitrary content (`<p>`, `<ul>`, for example) for the block that is hidden. `<summary>` is always the first child element; otherwise the browser creates one automatically. The open attribute makes the accordion expanded by default.","The main child tags\\nThe `<details>` element is intended for creating an expandable block (an accordion) without JavaScript. It supports two main child tags:\\n\\n- `<summary>` — a mandatory tag that sets the visible heading. If you do not provide one, the browser adds the text \\"Details\\" automatically.\\n- Any other HTML tags — the content that will be hidden or shown when `<summary>` is clicked. These may be `<p>`, `<ul>`, `<div>`, `<img>` and so on.\\nA code example\\n\\nThe open attribute\\nTo have the accordion expanded initially, add the `open` attribute to `<details>`:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `<details>` and `<summary>` for simple accordions without JavaScript, when you need to hide additional information — an FAQ or spoilers, for example. This improves accessibility and simplifies the code.","<details>\\n  <summary>Click to find out more</summary>\\n  <p>This is the hidden text that appears on a click.</p>\\n  <ul>\\n    <li>Item 1</li>\\n    <li>Item 2</li>\\n  </ul>\\n</details>\\n\\n<details open>\\n  <summary>Already open</summary>\\n  <p>This block is visible straight away.</p>\\n</details>"],[278,"For a button that opens a modal window, use the `<button>` tag. It is semantically correct and accessible to the keyboard and to screen readers. Do not use a `<div>` or a `<span>` with click handlers, since they have no built-in accessibility.","The right HTML tag for a modal-window button\\nTo create a button that opens a modal window you should use the `<button>` tag. This tag semantically denotes an interactive element that performs an action when pressed. Unlike a `<div>` or a `<span>`, `<button>` is focusable by keyboard by default and is handled correctly by screen readers, which improves accessibility.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhy you should not use other tags\\n\\n- A `<div>` or a `<span>` has no built-in button role, which requires additional ARIA attributes (`role=\\"button\\"`, for example) and the manual handling of keyboard events.\\n- Using an `<a>` with href=\\"#\\" can lead to unwanted navigation or a changed URL unless the default behaviour is prevented.\\nConclusion\\nUse `<button>` for any action, opening modal windows included. This ensures semantic correctness, accessibility and ease of implementation.","<button id=\\"openModalBtn\\">Open the modal window</button>\\n\\n<div id=\\"myModal\\" class=\\"modal\\">\\n  <div class=\\"modal-content\\">\\n    <span class=\\"close\\">&times;</span>\\n    <p>The modal window\'s content.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<script>\\n  document.getElementById(\'openModalBtn\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\\n    document.getElementById(\'myModal\').style.display = \'block\';\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[279,"For a link-button that performs navigation on a page, use the `<a>` tag with an href attribute. It is the semantically correct element for moving between pages or to anchors. Do not use `<button>` for navigation, since it is intended for actions within a page.","The core concept\\nTo create a link that takes the user to another page or to an anchor on the current page, you should use the `<a>` (anchor) tag. This tag is the semantically correct HTML element for navigation. The `href` attribute states the target URL or the anchor\'s identifier (`#section`, for example).\\nWhy not `<button>`?\\nThe `<button>` tag is intended for performing actions within a page, such as submitting a form or calling a JavaScript function. Using `<button>` for navigation breaks HTML\'s semantics and can degrade accessibility for screen-reader users and for search engines.\\nA code example\\n\\nStyling it as a button\\nIf you need the link to look like a button, use CSS to style the `<a>` tag. This preserves the semantics and ensures the correct behaviour.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the `<a>` tag with an `href` attribute for any navigation on a page or between pages. This ensures semantic correctness, accessibility and correct indexing by search engines.","<!-- Correct: a link for navigation -->\\n<a href=\\"/about\\">About us</a>\\n\\n<!-- A link to an anchor on the same page -->\\n<a href=\\"#features\\">Go to the features</a>\\n\\n<!-- Incorrect: a button for navigation -->\\n<button onclick=\\"location.href=\'/about\'\\">About us</button>\\n\\n<a href=\\"/register\\" class=\\"button\\">Register</a>"],[280,"The `<details>` tag does not support animating its expansion and collapse through a CSS transition or animation, since its height changes instantly. For smooth animation you have to use JavaScript, which complicates the code. `<details>` also does not let you manage the state of several elements at once, which is necessary for an accordion.","Why `<details>` is not suitable for an animated accordion\\nThe `<details>` tag is a native HTML element for creating expandable content. Its behaviour does not meet the requirements of an animated accordion, however, for several reasons.\\nNo CSS animation\\nBrowsers do not support animating the `height` or `max-height` property on `<details>`, since its expansion happens instantly. A CSS transition does not fire, because the change of state is not gradual.\\n\\nManaging the state\\nAn accordion requires the other elements to close when one is opened. `<details>` provides no built-in mechanism for this — you have to use JavaScript to manage the `open` attribute on all the elements.\\n\\nStyling limitations\\nThe styling of `<details>` is limited: you cannot easily change the icon or animate the marker without additional CSS tricks. This makes a consistent design harder to achieve.\\nConclusion\\nUse `<details>` for simple expandable blocks without animation. For an accordion with smooth opening and closing and synchronized states, it is better to implement a custom component in JavaScript with a CSS transition.","/* The animation does not work */\\ndetails {\\n  transition: height 0.3s ease;\\n}\\ndetails[open] {\\n  height: auto;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of managing it by hand\\nconst details = document.querySelectorAll(\'details\');\\ndetails.forEach(d => {\\n  d.addEventListener(\'toggle\', () => {\\n    details.forEach(other => {\\n      if (other !== d) other.removeAttribute(\'open\');\\n    });\\n  });\\n});"],[281,"To implement an accordion without JavaScript you can use the `<details>` and `<summary>` tags. Every block of the accordion is wrapped in `<details>` and its heading in `<summary>`. When `<summary>` is clicked, the browser expands or hides the content of `<details>` automatically. This is the semantically correct, accessible way of creating simple accordions.","Using semantic tags for an accordion\\nFor creating an accordion without JavaScript the `<details>` and `<summary>` tags are ideally suited. They are part of HTML5 and provide built-in functionality for expanding and hiding content. This is semantically correct, since browsers and assistive technologies (screen readers, for example) interpret these elements properly.\\nHow it works\\nEvery section of the accordion is wrapped in `<details>`. Inside it the first child element is `<summary>`, which serves as the heading. The rest of the content stays hidden until a click. When `<summary>` is clicked, the browser toggles the `open` attribute on `<details>`, showing or hiding the content.\\nA code example\\n\\nParticulars and styling\\nBy default `<details>` is displayed as a block with a marker (a triangle) to the left of `<summary>`. You can style it with CSS — removing the marker or adding an animation, for example. For complex accordions where only one section may be open at a time, however, JavaScript will be needed, since HTML does not provide that functionality out of the box.\\nConclusion\\nThe `<details>` and `<summary>` tags are a simple, accessible way of creating accordions without JavaScript. They suit cases where no complex behaviour is required — an FAQ or documentation, for example. If finer control is needed, JavaScript solutions are worth considering.","<details>\\n  <summary>Section 1</summary>\\n  <p>The content of the first section.</p>\\n</details>\\n<details>\\n  <summary>Section 2</summary>\\n  <p>The content of the second section.</p>\\n</details>\\n<details>\\n  <summary>Section 3</summary>\\n  <p>The content of the third section.</p>\\n</details>"],[282,"HTTP status codes fall into five classes. The 4xx codes (400–499) denote client errors — 404 Not Found or 400 Bad Request, for example. The 5xx codes (500–599) indicate server errors, such as 500 Internal Server Error or 503 Service Unavailable. Understanding these ranges helps you determine quickly who is to blame for a problem — the client or the server.","The classification of HTTP status codes\\nHTTP status codes fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit. The code\'s first digit indicates the category of the response. For diagnosing errors the two most important ranges are 4xx (client errors) and 5xx (server errors).\\nClient errors (4xx)\\nThe range 400–499 means the request contains incorrect data or cannot be processed because of the client\'s actions. Examples:\\n\\n- 400 Bad Request — incorrect request syntax.\\n- 401 Unauthorized — authentication is required.\\n- 403 Forbidden — access is denied, even with authentication.\\n- 404 Not Found — the resource was not found.\\n- 429 Too Many Requests — the request limit has been exceeded.\\nServer errors (5xx)\\nThe range 500–599 indicates that the server could not fulfil the request because of an internal problem. Examples:\\n\\n- 500 Internal Server Error — a general server error.\\n- 502 Bad Gateway — an invalid response from an upstream server.\\n- 503 Service Unavailable — the server is temporarily unavailable (because of overload, for example).\\n- 504 Gateway Timeout — the wait for an upstream server timed out.\\nAn example of code handling statuses\\n\\nIn this example the code checks the status\'s range and prints the appropriate message. This helps a developer understand quickly where to look for the problem.\\nConclusion: knowing the ranges of HTTP status codes is necessary for debugging web applications effectively, for writing correct error handling on the client and the server, and for analysing logs and monitoring.","fetch(\'/api/data\')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (response.status >= 400 && response.status < 500) {\\n      console.error(\'A client error:\', response.status);\\n    } else if (response.status >= 500) {\\n      console.error(\'A server error:\', response.status);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error(\'A network error:\', error));"],[283,"An accordion without JavaScript is implemented with a hidden checkbox and a label. The checkbox holds the open/closed state, while the CSS selector :checked controls the display of the content. A CSS transition on the max-height or transform property is used for the animation. This is a simple, accessible way of creating interactive elements without scripts.","The principle of a CSS accordion\\nAn accordion without JavaScript is built on the \\"checkbox hack\\": a hidden `<input type=\\"checkbox\\">` holds the state (open or closed), while the `<label>` associated with it serves as the visible button. When the user clicks the label, the checkbox toggles, and the CSS selector `:checked` applies styles to the neighbouring elements, showing or hiding the content.\\nThe basic HTML structure\\n\\nThe CSS for the animation\\nFor smooth opening, the `max-height` property with a transition is used. Initially the content is hidden (`max-height: 0; overflow: hidden`), and when the checkbox is activated it expands to a large value.\\n\\nUses and limitations\\nThis approach suits simple interfaces where no complex logic or multiple open panels are required. For an accordion where only one section may be open, you can use `<input type=\\"radio\\">` elements with the same name. The method does not support animating the height precisely, however (because of the fixed max-height) and is awkward for dynamic content.\\nConclusion\\nA pure CSS accordion is an excellent solution for static pages where performance and the absence of JavaScript dependencies matter. It is simple to implement but limited in flexibility and in the precision of its animation.","<div class=\\"accordion\\">\\n  <input type=\\"checkbox\\" id=\\"item1\\" class=\\"accordion-input\\">\\n  <label for=\\"item1\\" class=\\"accordion-label\\">Heading 1</label>\\n  <div class=\\"accordion-content\\">\\n    <p>The panel\'s content.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n.accordion-content {\\n  max-height: 0;\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n  transition: max-height 0.3s ease;\\n}\\n\\n.accordion-input:checked ~ .accordion-content {\\n  max-height: 200px; /* a sufficiently large value */\\n}"],[284,"onSubmit is the event that fires when a form is submitted — by pressing a submit button or the Enter key, for example. If it does not fire, the possible causes are: the button has no type=\\"submit\\", the form is nested inside another form, or there is a JavaScript error in the handler. It is also worth checking that the event has not been cancelled with preventDefault unnecessarily.","How onSubmit works\\nThe onSubmit event on an HTML form arises when the user tries to send the data — by pressing a button with type=\\"submit\\" or the Enter key inside an input field, for example. A handler for this event lets you intercept the submission, perform validation or an asynchronous request, and then either allow the submission or cancel it with the event.preventDefault() method.\\nWhy it might not fire\\n\\n- The submit button has no type=\\"submit\\" attribute (the default is type=\\"button\\" in some browsers).\\n- The form is nested inside another form — nested forms are not supported in HTML.\\n- An error in the handler\'s JavaScript code that interrupts execution.\\n- The use of event.preventDefault() without a subsequent submission.\\n- The input field is not inside the `<form>` tag.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nonSubmit is a key tool for controlling the submission of forms in web applications. It is worth applying to validate data on the client, to submit asynchronously without reloading the page and to improve the user experience. Always check the form\'s structure and that the button has type=\\"submit\\", in order to avoid problems.","document.getElementById(\'myForm\').addEventListener(\'submit\', function(event) {\\n  event.preventDefault(); // we cancel the standard submission\\n  const data = new FormData(event.target);\\n  console.log(\'The form data:\', Object.fromEntries(data));\\n  // here the data could be sent through fetch\\n});"],[285,"To close a modal window by clicking the background you need to add a click handler to the overlay (the darkened background). Inside the handler you check that the click was on the overlay itself rather than on the modal window\'s content. This can be done by comparing event.target with the overlay element, or by using event.stopPropagation() on the modal window\'s content.","The concept of closing a modal window by clicking the background\\nWhen a user clicks the darkened background (the overlay) of a modal window, they expect the window to close. This is a standard UX pattern that improves interaction with an interface. The implementation is based on handling click events and the bubbling of events in the DOM.\\nHow it works\\nThe overlay is the parent element, which takes up the whole screen. The modal window sits inside the overlay. When the overlay is clicked, the event bubbles, and we can intercept it at the overlay\'s level. A click on the modal window\'s content also bubbles up to the overlay, however, so we have to distinguish whether the click was on the overlay itself rather than on its child elements.\\nAn example implementation\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Web applications with modal windows, dialogs and pop-ups.\\n- UI libraries (React, Vue) often provide built-in solutions.\\n- Custom components for improving UX.\\nConclusion\\nUse a check of event.target, or stopPropagation, for reliably closing a modal window on a click on the background. It is a simple, effective way of improving the user\'s interaction with an interface.","// HTML: <div id=\\"overlay\\"><div id=\\"modal\\">...</div></div>\\n\\nconst overlay = document.getElementById(\'overlay\');\\nconst modal = document.getElementById(\'modal\');\\n\\noverlay.addEventListener(\'click\', (event) => {\\n  // If the click was on the overlay itself rather than on the modal\\n  if (event.target === overlay) {\\n    closeModal();\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// The alternative approach: stop the bubbling inside the modal\\nmodal.addEventListener(\'click\', (event) => {\\n  event.stopPropagation();\\n});\\n\\noverlay.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n  closeModal();\\n});"],[286,"The click event fires when an element is clicked with the mouse, while the input event fires when the value of an input element (a text field, for example) changes. Click is used for actions such as submitting a form, while input is for tracking every change to the input in real time.","The difference between the click and input events\\nThe `click` and `input` events belong to different kinds of interaction between the user and a web page. `click` arises when a mouse button is pressed and released on an element, while `input` fires when the value of a form element such as a text field or a slider changes.\\nWhen and how they are applied\\nThe `click` event is often used for buttons, links and other interactive elements, where a response to a completed action is required. The `input` event is applied to input fields in order to respond to every change in the text — for autocompletion or real-time validation, for example.\\nCode examples\\n\\nIn the first example the message appears only after a click, while in the second every change to the text is printed to the console.\\nConclusion\\nUse `click` for actions that require explicit confirmation, and `input` for an immediate response to the data being entered — in search suggestions or forms with dynamic validation, for example.","// A click handler for a button\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myButton\').addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\\n  alert(\'The button was pressed!\');\\n});\\n\\n// An input handler for a text field\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myInput\').addEventListener(\'input\', function() {\\n  console.log(\'The current value:\', this.value);\\n});"],[287,"The \'input\' event fires every time a text field\'s value changes, including pasting, deleting or a change through autofill. Unlike \'change\', it does not wait for focus to be lost. It is the main event for updating data reactively in real time.","What is the input event?\\nThe `input` event is a standard DOM event that arises on every change to the value of an `<input>` or `<textarea>` element. It fires immediately after the user has typed, deleted or pasted a character, including actions through the context menu or the browser\'s autofill.\\nThe difference from the other events\\n\\n- `change` — fires only after focus has been lost and only if the value changed. It is not suitable for an immediate response.\\n- `keydown` / `keyup` — they respond to key presses but do not account for pasting with the mouse or autofill.\\n- `input` — universal and reliable for tracking any change to the value.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nIn this example every change in the field prints the new value to the console. This is useful for search-as-you-type, real-time validation or counting characters.\\nConclusion\\nThe `input` event is the main tool for responding to every change in a text field. It should be applied wherever immediate feedback from the user\'s input is needed — in autocompletes, filters or forms with dynamic validation, for example.","const input = document.getElementById(\'myInput\');\\ninput.addEventListener(\'input\', function(event) {\\n  console.log(\'The current value:\', event.target.value);\\n});"],[288,"The controller in MVC is the component that handles the user\'s input, interacts with the model to update the data and passes the result to the view. It acts as an intermediary between the model and the view, managing the application\'s logic. When a button is pressed, for example, the controller calls a method on the model and updates the interface.","What is the controller in MVC?\\nThe controller is the central element of the Model-View-Controller pattern, responsible for handling the user\'s actions and coordinating between the model and the view. It receives requests (clicks or keyboard input, for example), interprets them and decides which logic to perform. The controller does not contain business logic directly but delegates it to the model, after which it updates the view.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the user interacts with the interface, the controller receives the event, calls the appropriate method on the model to change the data, and then notifies the view that it needs repainting. This ensures a separation of concerns: the model manages the data, the view the display, and the controller the connection between them.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nMVC is widely used in web frameworks (Ruby on Rails, Django, Spring MVC, for example) and in developing desktop applications. The controller simplifies the testing and maintenance of code, since the input logic is separated from the data and the interface.\\nConclusion: the controller in MVC is necessary for organizing a clean separation of concerns, which makes an application more modular and easier to extend. It is worth applying in projects where structure and scalability matter.","// A controller for managing users\\nclass UserController {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n  }\\n\\n  // Handling the addition of a user\\n  addUser(name) {\\n    this.model.createUser(name);\\n    this.view.renderUserList(this.model.getUsers());\\n  }\\n\\n  // Handling the deletion of a user\\n  deleteUser(id) {\\n    this.model.removeUser(id);\\n    this.view.renderUserList(this.model.getUsers());\\n  }\\n}"],[289,"A URL consists of several logical parts: the protocol (https, for example), the domain (or IP address), the port (optional), the path to the resource, the query string (the query parameters) and the fragment (the anchor). These parts let you state exactly where and how to get a resource on the network.","The structure of a URL/URI\\nA URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is a resource\'s address on the network. It consists of several logical parts, each of which performs its own function. Understanding this structure is necessary for working properly with HTTP requests, APIs and web development in general.\\nThe main components\\n\\n- The protocol (scheme) — it determines the way the data is transmitted: `http`, `https`, `ftp`, for example.\\n- The domain (host) — the server\'s name or IP address: `example.com`, for example.\\n- The port — an optional element stating the port number on the server (80 by default for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).\\n- The path — it states the resource\'s location on the server: `/api/users`, for example.\\n- The query string — it begins with `?` and contains parameters in the form `key=value`, separated by `&`.\\n- The fragment — it begins with `#` and points to a particular part of the resource (a section on a page, for example).\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the protocol is `https`, the domain `api.example.com`, the port `8080`, the path `/users`, the query string `page=1&limit=10` and the fragment `section2`.\\nUse\\nKnowing a URL\'s structure helps you form requests to an API properly, handle the parameters on the server and the client, and understand how a browser or HTTP client interprets an address. When developing a REST API, for example, it is important to divide the path and the query parameters correctly.\\nConclusion: understanding a URL\'s components is necessary for any work with web technologies, from simply following links to the complex integration of microservices.","https://api.example.com:8080/users?page=1&limit=10#section2"],[290,"An attached property is a special kind of dependency property that is defined in one class but can be set on any other element. It lets child elements set the values of properties affecting their position or behaviour within a parent container. The Grid.Row property, for example, sets the grid row for an element, even though the property itself is defined in the Grid class.","What are attached properties?\\nAttached properties are a mechanism in WPF that lets one element set the values of properties defined in another class. It is an extension of the dependency property system, in which a property is declared in one type but can be set on any object that supports dependency properties.\\nHow does it work?\\nAttached properties are implemented through static Set and Get methods, which call the internal DependencyObject.SetValue and GetValue methods. In XAML they are written in the form `OwnerClass.PropertyName`. The `Grid.Row` property, for example, lets you set the grid row for any child element.\\n\\nAn example of use in XAML\\n\\nHere `Grid.Row` and `Grid.Column` are attached properties that determine the button\'s position in the grid.\\nWhere is it applied?\\nAttached properties are widely used in layout controls (Grid, Canvas, DockPanel) and also in styles and triggers. They let you create flexible, reusable components without breaking the class hierarchy.\\nConclusion: attached properties are a powerful WPF tool for dividing logic between parent and child elements. They are worth applying when you need to set an element\'s behaviour or position in the context of another container without changing the element itself.","// Defining an attached property\\npublic static readonly DependencyProperty RowProperty =\\n    DependencyProperty.RegisterAttached(\\n        \\"Row\\",\\n        typeof(int),\\n        typeof(Grid),\\n        new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(0));\\n\\npublic static void SetRow(UIElement element, int value)\\n{\\n    element.SetValue(RowProperty, value);\\n}\\n\\npublic static int GetRow(UIElement element)\\n{\\n    return (int)element.GetValue(RowProperty);\\n}\\n\\n<Grid>\\n    <Button Grid.Row=\\"1\\" Grid.Column=\\"2\\" Content=\\"Click\\" />\\n</Grid>"],[291,"No, changing the transform or translate properties does not cause a reflow. These properties are handled at the compositing stage, bypassing the layout and paint stages. This makes them ideal for animations, since they do not recompute the geometry of other elements and do not cause expensive repaints.","What a reflow is and how it works\\nA reflow (or layout) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the geometry of elements: their dimensions, their position and their placement relative to other elements. A reflow is triggered by changes that affect the page\'s layout — a change to the width, the height, the margin, the padding, display, positioning (top, left) and so on. It is an expensive operation, since it can affect both child and parent elements.\\nWhy do transform/translate not cause a reflow?\\nThe transform and translate properties (and also opacity, filter and will-change) are handled at the compositing stage. The browser creates a separate layer for the element (a compositing layer) and moves it with the GPU, without recomputing layout and paint. This means a change to transform does not affect the position of the other elements in the document\'s flow — the element \\"floats\\" above the page, as it were.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhen does this matter?\\nWhen creating animations, especially at 60fps, using transform rather than left/top is critically important for performance. It is standard practice in CSS animations and in JavaScript libraries (GreenSock, Framer Motion, for example).\\nConclusion: use transform/translate for animations of movement in order to avoid a reflow and keep the interface running smoothly, especially on mobile devices and with complex layouts.","// Bad: it causes a reflow on every frame of the animation\\nconst el = document.getElementById(\'box\');\\nel.style.left = \'100px\'; // A reflow!\\n\\n// Good: it causes no reflow and uses compositing\\nel.style.transform = \'translateX(100px)\'; // No reflow"],[292,"A chart\'s theme in Chart.js can be customized through the global settings in Chart.defaults, overriding colours, fonts and styles. You can also use built-in plugins such as Chart.plugins.register, or create your own. For individual charts the settings are passed in the options object when the instance is created.","The main ways of customizing a theme in Chart.js\\nChart.js provides flexible capabilities for changing a chart\'s appearance, which lets you adapt it to a corporate style or to specific design requirements. Customization can be done at several levels: globally, for a particular chart, or through plugins.\\nConfiguring the theme globally\\nThe global settings are set through the `Chart.defaults` object. This lets you change the colours, fonts and other parameters for every chart on a page. For example:\\n\\nConfiguring a particular chart\\nWhen creating a chart you can pass an `options` object with individual settings. This overrides the global values. An example:\\n\\nUsing plugins\\nChart.js supports plugins that can change the theme. The `chartjs-plugin-datalabels` plugin, for example, lets you configure data labels. You can also create your own plugin for unique styling:\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustomizing a theme in Chart.js is done through the global settings, the parameters of a particular chart, or plugins. This lets you control the appearance of your visualizations flexibly, making them match your design and brand requirements.","Chart.defaults.color = \'#333\';\\nChart.defaults.font.family = \'Arial\';\\nChart.defaults.borderColor = \'#ccc\';\\n\\nconst ctx = document.getElementById(\'myChart\').getContext(\'2d\');\\nnew Chart(ctx, {\\n  type: \'bar\',\\n  data: { ... },\\n  options: {\\n    plugins: {\\n      legend: {\\n        labels: {\\n          color: \'#ff0000\'\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    scales: {\\n      y: {\\n        grid: {\\n          color: \'#e0e0e0\'\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nChart.register({\\n  id: \'customTheme\',\\n  beforeDraw: function(chart) {\\n    const ctx = chart.ctx;\\n    ctx.fillStyle = \'#f5f5f5\';\\n    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, chart.width, chart.height);\\n  }\\n});"],[293,"Reflow and repaint are stages of the browser\'s rendering. A reflow happens when elements\' geometry changes, a repaint when styles that do not affect dimensions change. Optimization involves minimizing changes to the DOM, using classes rather than inline styles, applying `will-change`, `transform` and `opacity` for animations, and batching the reading and writing of properties.","What are reflow and repaint?\\nA reflow (or layout) is the process of computing the dimensions and positions of the elements on a page. It is triggered by any change that affects the geometry: a change to the width or height, adding or removing elements, changing the font and so on. A repaint is the process of drawing the pixels, which happens after a reflow or when visual properties that do not affect the geometry change (a background colour or a shadow, for example).\\nThe main ways of optimizing\\n\\n- Minimizing reflows: avoid frequent changes that cause the layout to be recomputed. Instead of changing styles one at a time, for example, use classes or `cssText`.\\n- Batching reads and writes: reading properties that cause a reflow (`offsetHeight`, for example) after a write can lead to a forced reflow. Group your reads and writes separately.\\n- Using `transform` and `opacity`: these properties are handled at the compositing level and cause neither a reflow nor a repaint, which is ideal for animations.\\n- Applying `will-change`: it hints to the browser that an element is going to change, letting it create a separate layer in advance.\\n- Avoiding complex selectors: although this affects repaint less, complex selectors can slow the recomputation of styles down.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimizing reflow and repaint is critical for the performance of web applications, especially with animations and dynamic content. Use modern CSS properties, group your operations and avoid forced layouts, for a smooth interface.","// Bad: it causes a reflow on every iteration\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\\n  const el = document.getElementById(\'item\');\\n  el.style.width = i + \'px\';\\n  console.log(el.offsetWidth); // a forced reflow\\n}\\n\\n// Good: batched reading and writing\\nconst el = document.getElementById(\'item\');\\nconst widths = [];\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\\n  widths.push(i);\\n}\\nfor (let w of widths) {\\n  el.style.width = w + \'px\';\\n}\\n// Reading after all the writes\\nconsole.log(el.offsetWidth);"],[294,"Square brackets in JSON denote an array — an ordered list of values. An array may contain any data type: strings, numbers, objects, other arrays. The elements are separated by commas.","What are square brackets in JSON?\\nIn JSON the square brackets `[]` are used to denote an array. An array is an ordered collection of values that may contain elements of any type: strings, numbers, objects, booleans, `null` or other arrays.\\nThe syntax and examples\\nAn array begins with `[` and ends with `]`. The elements are separated by commas. An example of a simple array of strings:\\n\\nAn array may contain different data types:\\n\\nArrays can also be nested:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nArrays in JSON are widely used to pass lists of data — a list of users, products or messages, for example. They are the basis for structuring data in APIs and configuration files.\\nConclusion\\nSquare brackets in JSON denote an array, which lets you store ordered collections of data. It is a key element of JSON\'s syntax, necessary for working with any list in web development and data exchange.","[\\"apple\\", \\"banana\\", \\"cherry\\"]\\n\\n[42, \\"hello\\", true, null, {\\"key\\": \\"value\\"}]\\n\\n[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]"],[295,"The Network tab in the browser\'s developer tools shows every network request being sent and received. You can see the headers, the request and response bodies, the status codes, the durations and the size of the data. This helps you debug problems with the loading of resources, with API requests and with performance.","Where to look at network requests in DevTools\\nIn the browser\'s developer tools (DevTools) the Network tab is responsible for analysing network activity. It displays every HTTP request the page sends: the loading of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images and fonts, plus XHR/Fetch requests to APIs.\\nHow to use it\\nOpen DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I), go to the Network tab and reload the page. You will see a table of requests, where you can:\\n\\n- Filter by type (XHR, JS, CSS, Img, Doc and so on)\\n- Inspect the request\'s and response\'s headers\\n- Look at the response body and the data that was sent (the payload)\\n- Assess the duration (Timing) and the size\\nAn example of debugging\\nSuppose you expect a POST request to be sent when a button is pressed. Open Network and press the button — a new request will appear. Select it and go to the Payload tab in order to see the data that was sent, and to Response in order to check the server\'s reply.\\n\\nIn Network you will see this request, its status (200, 404, 500 and so on), its duration and its content.\\nConclusion\\nThe Network tab is an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction, checking that API requests are correct, analysing the loading performance of resources and hunting for errors on the client\'s or the server\'s side.","// An example: sending a POST request through fetch\\nfetch(\'/api/data\', {\\n  method: \'POST\',\\n  headers: { \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ key: \'value\' })\\n})\\n.then(res => res.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));"],[296,"A native mobile application is built specifically for one platform, such as iOS or Android, using that platform\'s own languages and tools (Swift/Objective-C for iOS, Kotlin/Java for Android). It has direct access to the device\'s hardware capabilities, providing maximum performance and a smooth user interface. Such applications are installed through the official app stores and can use every function of the OS.","What is a native mobile application?\\nA native mobile application is a program developed to run on a particular operating system, such as iOS or Android, using the official programming languages and tools the platform provides. For iOS that means Swift or Objective-C and the Xcode environment; for Android, Kotlin or Java and Android Studio. The main advantage of the native approach is complete integration with the device and its capabilities.\\nThe key characteristics\\n\\n- Direct access to hardware components: the camera, GPS, the accelerometer, the sensors.\\n- High performance, since the code is compiled for the particular architecture.\\n- The use of every capability of the OS: push notifications, widgets, background tasks.\\n- Conformity with the platform\'s guidelines (the Human Interface Guidelines for iOS, Material Design for Android).\\nA code example (iOS, Swift)\\n\\nA code example (Android, Kotlin)\\n\\nWhen should you choose native development?\\nThe native approach is ideal for applications that require high performance (games, graphics editors), deep integration with the device (fitness trackers, cameras) or the use of specific platform features. It is also preferable when the flawless operation of the interface and conformity with the platform\'s standards matter. Native development requires separate codebases for each platform, however, which increases the time and cost of development.\\nConclusion: native applications provide the best user experience and performance, but they require more resources than cross-platform solutions.","import UIKit\\n\\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\\n        super.viewDidLoad()\\n        // Configuring the interface\\n        let label = UILabel()\\n        label.text = \\"Hello, world!\\"\\n        label.textAlignment = .center\\n        view.addSubview(label)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimport android.os.Bundle\\nimport androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity\\nimport android.widget.TextView\\n\\nclass MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)\\n        val textView = findViewById<TextView>(R.id.textView)\\n        textView.text = \\"Hello, world!\\"\\n    }\\n}"],[297,"JSON supports six types of value: string, number, boolean, null, object and array. Strings are always in double quotes; numbers may be integers or floating-point. Objects and arrays can be nested.","The types of value in JSON\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript\'s syntax. It supports a strictly limited set of data types, which makes it universal and simple to parse in any programming language.\\nThe list of supported types\\n\\n- String — a sequence of Unicode characters enclosed in double quotes. Example: `\\"hello\\"`. Special characters such as `\\\\\\"` or `\\\\n` must be escaped.\\n- Number — an integer or a floating-point number. It uses no quotes. Examples: `42`, `-3.14`, `1.5e10`. It does not support NaN, Infinity or leading zeros.\\n- Boolean — `true` or `false`, without quotes.\\n- null — a special value denoting the absence of data. It is written as `null`.\\n- Object — an unordered set of key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces `{}`. The keys are always strings in double quotes.\\n- Array — an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets `[]`. The elements may be of any type, other arrays or objects included.\\nAn example of valid JSON\\n\\nWhat is not supported\\nJSON does not support dates, functions, undefined, symbols or comments. Dates are normally passed as strings in ISO 8601 format. This limitation makes JSON safe to pass between systems.\\nConclusion: knowing JSON\'s types is necessary for serializing data correctly and for writing APIs and configuration files. Always check that your data conforms to these six types, in order to avoid parsing errors.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false,\\n  \\"address\\": null,\\n  \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", 42, true],\\n  \\"metadata\\": {\\n    \\"created\\": \\"2023-01-01\\"\\n  }\\n}"],[298,"Swagger is a tool for describing REST APIs in the OpenAPI format. It lets you generate interactive documentation automatically, where you can send requests and see the responses. The documentation describes the endpoints, the parameters, the headers, the request bodies and the response formats. Swagger makes interaction between developers and testers easier and also lets you automate testing.","What Swagger is and why it is needed\\nSwagger (now part of the OpenAPI specification) is a standard for describing REST APIs. It is a YAML or JSON file containing complete information about all of an API\'s endpoints: the methods, the paths, the parameters, the headers, the request bodies, the response codes and the data schemas. Swagger UI is a tool that visualizes that description as an interactive web page where you can send requests to the API and see the responses right in the browser.\\nHow do you use Swagger?\\nThe main scenarios of use:\\n\\n- Development: Swagger serves as the single source of truth for the frontend and the backend. A frontend developer can see what data the API expects without waiting for the backend to be ready.\\n- Testing: Swagger UI lets you check the endpoints quickly without writing code. There are also tools for generating tests automatically from a Swagger specification.\\n- Documentation: Swagger generates readable documentation automatically, which is always up to date as long as the specification is kept current.\\nA code example (a fragment of a Swagger specification in YAML)\\n\\nConclusion\\nSwagger is an indispensable tool for developing REST APIs as a team. It improves communication, speeds up development and testing, and provides up-to-date documentation. It is worth applying in any project that has an API, especially when working in a distributed team or when integration with external services is required.","openapi: 3.0.0\\ninfo:\\n  title: User API\\n  version: 1.0.0\\npaths:\\n  /users/{id}:\\n    get:\\n      summary: Get user by ID\\n      parameters:\\n        - name: id\\n          in: path\\n          required: true\\n          schema:\\n            type: integer\\n      responses:\\n        \'200\':\\n          description: Successful response\\n          content:\\n            application/json:\\n              schema:\\n                type: object\\n                properties:\\n                  id:\\n                    type: integer\\n                  name:\\n                    type: string"],[299,"For a comment entity you have to store a unique identifier, the comment\'s text, the date and time it was created, the author\'s identifier and the identifier of the parent entity (a post, for example). You can also add an edited flag, likes and the identifier of a parent comment for nesting. This data provides the basic functionality and the links to the other entities.","Determining the data for a comment entity\\nWhen designing the data structure for a comment you have to consider its role in the system: a comment links a user to some content and may support nesting. The main fields include a unique identifier (id), the text (body), the creation date (created_at), the author\'s identifier (author_id) and the parent entity\'s identifier (post_id). In addition you can store the date it was edited (updated_at), the number of likes (likes_count) and the identifier of a parent comment (parent_id) for a tree structure.\\nAn example structure in SQL\\n\\nUse\\nSuch a structure is used in blogs, forums and social networks. The parent_id field lets you implement nested comments without additional tables. Indexes on author_id and post_id speed queries up. Conclusion: store the minimum fields necessary for the functionality, adding optional ones as the need arises.","CREATE TABLE comments (\\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\\n  body TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW(),\\n  updated_at TIMESTAMP,\\n  author_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id),\\n  post_id INTEGER REFERENCES posts(id),\\n  parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES comments(id),\\n  likes_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0\\n);"],[300,"S3 stores static files in the cloud, while a CDN caches them on servers located closer to the users. When a user requests a file, the CDN serves it from the nearest edge server rather than from the central store. This reduces latency and speeds up the loading of a site or application.","How S3 and a CDN solve the problem of geographical distance\\nWhen the server is far from the user, every request passes through a great many network nodes, which increases latency. This is especially critical for static content: images, CSS, JavaScript, video. S3 (Simple Storage Service) is object storage that keeps files in a particular region. If all the users are far from that region, loading will be slow.\\nThe role of a CDN\\nA CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a network of servers distributed all over the world. It caches content from S3 on edge servers located closer to the end users. When a user requests a file, the CDN routes the request to the nearest server that has a copy automatically. If there is no copy, the CDN fetches it from S3 and stores it for later requests.\\nAn example configuration\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- Lower latency: the content is delivered from the nearest server.\\n- Less load on S3: the requests are handled by the CDN.\\n- Saved bandwidth: a CDN can compress data and use HTTP/2.\\nConclusion: using S3 together with a CDN is the standard approach for global applications where the loading speed of static assets matters. It is especially useful for sites with an audience in different countries, for streaming services and for large online shops.","// An example: attaching CloudFront (a CDN) to an S3 bucket\\n// 1. You create an S3 bucket and upload the files\\n// 2. You create a CloudFront distribution\\n// 3. You name the origin — your S3 bucket\\n// 4. CloudFront serves the content through its edge servers automatically\\n\\n// An example URL after the configuration:\\n// https://d123.cloudfront.net/images/logo.png\\n// Instead of the direct S3 URL:\\n// https://my-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/images/logo.png"]]');export{e as default};